      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>-- __— Royalty and the ChristmasHolidays.—Princess Henry ' of Rattenberg is the only member a the Roval Family who keeps up the Victorian custom of spending Christ- as in the isle of Wight, and as usual the Princess and , her daughter Ena have been staying this Christmas at Osborne Cottage. The King and Queen have never departed from their long-established practice of keeping , Christmas at Sandringham, and this 3-ear the Royal circle at home is diminished, not only by the absence of the Prince and Princess of Wales in India, but also by the departure last Saturday of the Duke and Duchess of C,onnaught and Princess Patricia for South Africa. The Duke and Duchess contemplate making an extensive tour in South Africa, and the Royal party will be away for about four months. The King's arrangements will be influenced somewhat by the Parliamentary situation, but his Majesty also will us going abroad in the spring when his Majesty will return the recent visit of the King of Spain. King Edward has not been in Madrid I for thirty years, but almost the last official visit paid by I Queen Victoria was to the Queen Regent of Spain at Ban Sebastian. The Chatsworth Theatricals.—The Duke and Duchess of Devonshire always keep Christmas in true repre- sentative fashion at Chatsworth, N here, next week, they will entertain the King and Queen and a large house party. A new feature in the theatrical enter- tainments which are invariably included in the New Year iestivities at the " Palace of the Peak," will be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>EARLY- DAYS.</head>
        <p>EARLY- DAYS.</p>
        <p>How fiondly still we love to dwell On days and years long, long ago ; And trace the scenes. we knew so well,</p>
        <p>While tears unbidden softly flow. How happy were oar childish years Through love maternal, ever wise ; And April-like our childish tears, Were soon replaced by laughing eyes. 0 then how wonderful we deemed</p>
        <p>Earth's hills and valleys round us spread ; And wondrous too the sunlight seemed, Which on them all its glory shed. And 0, 'twas joy for ever new, With eager feet the moon to chase, As past the rolling clouds she flew, - And left us panting in the race. When school had ended for the day, In happy hands which knew no care, Through sylvan glades we loved to stray; An deck our brows with garlands there. And when deep snow lay on the fields, . . .</p>
