        <p>The Wicked Lady Howe rd;</p>
        <p>OR. . . The Coach Made of Dead Men's Bones. q •••• 2</p>
        <p>By SKIPP BORLPLSE, Author of 'A Bride from the Dead," "The Spectre Horseman," "The Shrieking Skull," "The Changed Brides," etc. etc.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER 1.</p>
        <p>"I INTEND TO KILL YOU DURING THE HAPPIEST</p>
        <p>11011 R OF'YOUIt LIFE!" It is the Christmas Eve of the year 1643, and the annual ball is in full swing in the great assembly room of the quaint old ooroughtown of Tavistock, in Devon, a room which had once been the refectory of its noble abbey of Saint Mary the Virgin and St. Rumen, and it was a common belief of that day that on such festive occasions as the present the ghost of one or other of the for long dead Black Monks would glide, mist like, amongst the guests, with stern visage and clasped hands, whenever one of them was destined to die a violent death within the follow- ing year. Upon the present occasion, however, not one amongst the two hundred or so of dancers gave a thought to the ancient Benedictine Brothers, whose once holy home they were profaning with their frivolous pleasures ; and yet not all of those lovely girls and handsome cavaliers were so happy as they seemed to be, for Violet Fitz- roy, the acknowledged belle of the assembly, was almost monopolised by middle-aged Lord Howard, whom she had consented to marry on account of his title and his wealth, whereas, in secret, she hated him with all her heart, whilst the young cornet of dragoons whom she loved with an equal degree of passion was powerless to worship her, save at a distance, and so found models are somewhat small, and tilted at the back and nothing better to do than to gnaw his amber- side in the most exaggerated style. The majority of hued rdoustachios, play with his sword hilt, and the hats are impossible without a bunch ; or "garland" wish, over and over to himself, that he could °feuds, to soften the oetline, and these are usually secured to the hat. A Society woman was overheard plunge the blade of the weapon guard-deep into the other day,debriting the vexed question as to whether his successful rival's body. it is better to fasten the"acquired "curls to the hat or How he cursed his fate in that lie was a the head. In the former instance, she remarked, it is younger son and a mere cornet of dragoons, with true that one sometimes feels embarrassed wheu it more gold lace upon his brilliant uniform than becomes unexpectedly necessary to remove ones head- gold coin in his purse ; aye, he would willingly gear, but in the latter ease, she went on to say it is have sold his very soul to the foul fiend at this almost impossible to remove one's hat, as the bunch of moment to become suddenly as rich as Lord curls appears ridiculous without the headgear it is Howard. who was owner of the great estates of intended to support. Oakhampton Park and Fitzford Chase, with a rent-roll of some thirty thousand pounds a year; for well he knew that in Such case the lovely Violet would have cast off her middle-aged suitor as carelessly as though he had been a split glove, and bestowed on him, the penniless corfiet, her person as well as her heart. And Lord Howard, unhappily for his peace of mind, was quite as well aware of this as ever his rival was, and being of a highly jealous disposi- tion he gave back Hubert Molyneux hate for hate, even though he knew that he held thigame in his own band, for he had long ago discovered that Violet Fitzroy loved rank and wealth far more than she was capable of loving a mere man, and these he could offer her whilst Moly- neux could not. Yet, for all that, it maddened him to perceive</p>
        <p>unearthly visitant; but Violet and Lord Howard not only beheld the monk but also fully understood his grim warning. Alas! well would it have been for both of them had they not so soon forgotten or dis- regarded it.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER 11. IN WHICH VIOLET "FLAUNTS IT AS MY LADY. HOWARD."</p>
        <p>rolled away in the direction ot CJakhampton Park and—dedtruction.</p>
        <p>The Christmas of 1643 has passed away. New Year's Eve has arrived, and with it 41, clear, frosty, moonlight night. ! Under the shadow of Betsy Grimehaw's • Tower, so-called because of the murder of a young woman of that name therein, whose ghost, on the night of "All Souls," is still believed to haunt that now detached remnant of the ancient Abbey, Cornet Hubert Molyncue is anxiously keeping tryst for the fair Violet Fitzroy, and as the clock of St. Eestachius' Church strikes the hour of nine, the blue-eyed, golden-haired girl, warmly clad in furs, hastens towards and joins him. • A silent embrace, and she demands of him eagerly— ! "Why did you insist upon my meeting you here to-night, Hubert, for you must know that, as the betrothed wife of Lord ,Howard, it was most unfitting that I should coma," "D— Lord Howard," was Molyneux's angry retort. You have come here to hear me vow that you shall never be his wife, inasmuch as I intend to kill him on hie very wedding eve. I gave him the promise that I would do so in wild, rugged, and lonely one, skirting, as it the presence of a crowd of witnesses at the , does, the dreary lower reaches of Dartmoor. Assembly Ball, and as a man of honour I can- But Lord Howard reeked naught of this, for not break my word." the carriage was a.s brilliantly lighted within as "And do you feel sure that you can kill him, it was without, and his beautiful girl wife lay in Hubert?" his arms, with her plump and snow-white neck "Well, as lam the hest swordsman in the ' and shoulders .all bare, and her soft, round King's Dragoons, I regard it as a dead cer- breaks rising and falling with the violence of the tainty, my dear girl." emotions under which she laboured. "And then you would fain marry me, I pro- " Violet," his lordship exclaimed at length, as some, and support me on your munificent pay he strained her to his embrace, "never before of five shillings a day,. for let me assure you have I seen you look so lovely. This—this is, that my father, were I to commit so rash an indeed, the happiest hour of my life." act, would turn me penniless from his doors." Scarcely had he uttered the words when a "Love will enable me to carve you a fortune harsh voice called upon the coachman to pull up, with my sword, Violet." three horsemen loomed out of the surrounding "What, in the service of a bankrupt King, gloom, and, a second later, one of these dis- to bolster, up whose fast-sinking cause ypur mounted, approached the carriage, lugged open father has ruined himself, like many another the nearest door thereof, and then doffing his gallant Cavalier? Your words are words -of plumed hat, and making its inmates a polite bow, folly. Hubert." - he exclaimed : "It seems to me that in your heart you are "My lord, you and I have a little account to bent mi marrying Lord, Howard, in which case settle which is just twelve months overdue, so if you could never have truly loved ins." you will kindly alight, my sword shall give you "On the contrary, I love you with every whit a quittance in full for the debt 60. long owing. as deep and true an affection as you can pos• My name, as you will doubtless remember, is sibly feel for me. Added to that I will become Hubert Molyneux." • - your wife if you will accept me as Lady Howard "Mr. Molyneux." answered his lordship instead of as Violet Fitzroy." stiffly—aye; as stiffly as his beautiful Wife now "What on earth do you mean? You speak sat upright in the carriage by his aide, with a in riddles." baleful light in her violet eves, "strange as is the "Riddles very easy to guess, I should think. time and .place that you 'have chosen for our Well, I will give you a clue to their answer. duello, I acknowledge that you are acting within Kill Lord Howard after he has married me your rights, and, therefore,l shall he most happy instead of before, and then you will wed a • to oblige you.,, • , wealthy widow instead of a penniless maid." So saying he stepped forth from the carriage to