      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S,A. At the usual meeting of the above Society, which was held on Sunday last. Ex-Sergeant Major Roberts pre- sided over a good attendance. 'Alr. E. Johnson, B.Ae of Manchester, was the speaker, and gave a most able and interesting address upon " The Star of Bethlehem." ; Be compared the celebrations at this great festival and I the Ronnsh authorities with'our own Christmas customs. ! Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac., presido at the organ. !Mr. W. J. Barnes sang " Nazareth " in a pleasing , manner. Special Christmas hymns wereleartily joined in, The Aisual hearty greetings were given by the Chairman, Speaker, and Secretary. Mr. A. Healey and Mr. A. Downes also took part in the meeting. I •</p>
        <p>P.S.A. Meetings. At the meeting of the Warrington P.S.X. Society which was held in the Co-operative Hall,Saukey-street on Sunday, Mt. Bißingham presided, and gave a short address pointing out that the only liie worth living was a life spent in fellowship with Jesus Christ. The Rev. J. F. Matthews delivered an earnest address on " Christ's abiding presence." He stated that the life Christ had been, and was the impulse nod power of all that was the noblest and best in human life. He Pointed out how mony things we should miss were it not for the coining of Jesus Clnist into the Woad, and in conclusion urged all present to make Christ their companion and guide, and then their lives would have pot been lived in vain. Miss Lily Fairhurst ably sang • solos "Be was despised " and " The Wondrous Cross," the tune of which was composed by Mr. Fred Podmore. Mr. F. Podmore was the accompanist. Mr. E. Saunders read the Scripture lesson. 'Mr. IL Nelson on Mr. Wynne Jones also took part in the meeting.</p>
        <p>Icotc:</p>
        <p>Mr. li. Vines is one of those sturdy "sons of the soil "who form the back-bone of Britain's greatness. All his life he has spent in the village of Preston, near Lynhani, North Wilts., and a chapter out of his career will touch tte heart of many readers. Interviewed by the" Wiltshire Times " reporter; Mr. Vine said "Three years ago I negan to have attacks of giddiness, accompanied by loss of appetite, a languid feeling, and violent pains in my head. 114 weakness at times was such that I would lay on the ground helpless. Many times I have gone from morning to night without touching food,for the continual sickness an:, nauseous feeling made the thoughts of food obnoxious to me. I saw a doctor but nothing had any permanentibeneficial effect. I was in despair of ever being well again, when I read of the wonderful merits of Bile Beans. I at once sent fora box, and after taking the beans for a short period I was quite a changed man. My appetite was restored, the dizzy and languid feelings went away, and my strength returned. I took altogether three boxes of beans. It is nearly a year ago since I took bile beans, and from that time I have never had a return of the biliousness. I can do my work Letter now than have been able to for years, and I never felt better in nav life.". It is by producing such marvellous cures as they have done in Mr. Vines' case, that "Bile Beans for Biliousness" have attained such world-wide popularity. What "Bile Beans for Biliousness" did for Mr. Vines. they will do in your case to.</p>
