      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>JANUARY.</head>
        <p>JANUARY.</p>
        <p>I.—General Stoessel proposed surrender to General Nogi. 2.—The king and Queen and Princess Victoria arrived at Chatsworth on a visit to the Duke and Duchess of Devonshire.</p>
        <p>3.—Lord Mount Stephen gave 1;200000 to the King's Hospital Fund. s.—Port Arthur handed over to the Japanese. 7.—The King and Queen drove from Chatsworth to Buxton. Stslybt idge Mr.J. F. Cheetham (R.), 402); Mr. J. Travis-Clegg (U.), :1078; majority, 951. Tidal wave on East Coast, great damage; Scarborough Pier swept away. 12,---Nogi reported that his spoils at Port Arthur included-50 permanent forts, b4O cannon, 82.070 shells. 30 tons of ammunition, four battle- ohips. two eruisers, and 14 gunboats and destroyers. Mile-end: Hon. H. Lawson (U.), 2138; Mr. B. S. Straus (IL). 20; majority, 78. 14.—Lord Roberts unveiled, in the crypt of St. Paul's Cathedral, a tablet to the memory of the journalists who lost their lives while acting as special correspon- dents of the Brit- ish and Colonial press and of the noNV 8 agencies during the South African war. lo.—Fierce gale and snowstorm : Damage on the coast. Colliery ancident near St. Helens: Five</p>
        <p>Bonma':</p>
        <p>men killed. 13 injured. 17. At the old Bailey, 11. rt. Jones, solicitor, sentenced to 18 months' imprisonment in the second division for defrauding ‘Vandsworth Guar- dians. 10.--Serions collision on the Midland 'Railway at Cudworth, near Barnsley ; seven persons killed, many injured. 20,—Marquess of Bath appointed Under-Secre- tary for linda. 21.--Ex plosion at Ebbw Colliery, near Llanelly: 10 men killed, eight injured. 21.-I'rinee of Wales arrived in Treland on a itisit to Lord Ardilann. Billiartla: John Roberts ud e a record break of 821 at fllasgow. 20.—Diamond weighing 3030 carats found in the Premier Mine. Dorset (N.): Mr. 11. W. NVills (R.). 42:ift; sir IL Baker (U.), 3330; majority, ft,oo. (Mr. IVingfield-I)igby ([IO. deceased). 31.--Prineess Victoria tinecesKr idly operated on for appendicitis. llooley-Lawson Case: Sentence of 12 months' impr:soluneut pa Aed on Lawson at the Old Bailey upheld on appeal. FEBRUARY. I.—Prince of Wale; present at a Lev6e at Dublin • Castle. Mr. Chamberlain addressed a incetiog at flidushorougli. Groat Gaddesden !lace. Hemel Hempstead, destroyed by fire. ince of Wales present at it Drawing Room at Dublin Cuutle. Sir V. Nicholson appointed Governcr of Gibraltar, %Ica Si,' G. White. Lord Emlithrucv appointed Secretary for Scotland. Grand Duke or II eNSP sod Prior-test.; Eleonore of Solnis-llobi-nsolins•Lieli married at Darmstadt. Ling held a Goenell at Buckingham Pelaee. afterwards leaving for Mentmore on a visit t,. Lord Hosels.ry. 7. —Deputation re the Unemployed to Mr. Bal- four. Nelson and Company. Ltd.: Order for corn- ult• 111.-1).1,00rd murder: At the Old Bailey, Ed ward I Imo ;son was s( ntenerd to death. waio, appointed Lord Warden of the Cimpie Ports. Find it was announced that Wt,mier Castle was to be thrown open to the 14.--Tho nocumponied by the Owen, opened Parliament in State. Kildare (N.): Mr. J. O'Connor (N.) unopposed: Mr. Leanly (N.) (Accessed. 10. Doke of Ssve.Colairg (Duke of Albany) betr..thed te Princess Vittoria Adelaide of Schlcswig-lbdstein-SonderhurgAiliicksburg. Two exphedons on suhtnarine " A 5" at Queenstown. 1-01h-Liectenant Shinnor and fire men killed, tin it Good sod 13 men injured. Message 011)14 ti from the King. Billiards: Stevenson Lrole 14 reeord break of 812. 17. ['he Grand Sergius wla driving toccarda the Krn-to- hit) Pvhiee at Mos- emv when a bcnib \ass thrown under his eat riage by the (wee mitts of a slis!ge ‘vhich hind polled op t.o niloVe It to pas.. TI.O (”O'l 'age was hlown to pieces. tool the &lt;ln am! loil;e was instititly killed. his .itt•;ver also rt-eeiv- ioyt fatal injeries. T lie murderers Iv( ro Drro sled, Pad sum or bolo is re- pork.) to haVO said: Ido not care. I have done toy job." 22.—Liverpool (Everton): Mr. Harmood-Banner (U.), ; Mr. 11. Aggs (R.), 2543—majority, 1311. Great lire in Long Acre—motor works destroyed. The Agit&amp; diamond at Christie's realised ..l.:5100. 23.—Piercing of the Simplon Tunnel completed. 25.—The King and Prince of Wales attended a football match at Queen's Mob, when the Army beat the Navy by 2 goals to 0. Betrothal announced of Princess Margaret of Connaught and Prince Gustavus Adolphus of Sweden. Football: England drew with Ireland (A); Ireland beat ficotland by 11 points to 5. MARCH. I.—Announced that Lord Selborne would sue- veed Lord Milner as High Comihissioner in South Africa.</p>
        <p>G RAN 11l DURg</p>
        <p>2.—Westmorland N.): Mr. L. Jones (R.), 22, Major G. Noble (U.), 2702 ; majority, 220. 4.—Buteshire (3rd): Mr. N. Lamont (R.), 1460; Mr. E. T. Salveson (U.), 1420—maiority, 34 (Mr. A. Graham Murray (U.) appointed' Lord President of the Court of Session.) 6.—Mn Wyndham resigned the post of Chief Secretary for Ireland. 11. Colliery disaster at Clydach Vale: South Wales: 32 lives lost. Football: Wales beat Ireland for the Rugby championship by 10 points to three. 12.—Battle of Muliden : Marshal Ovama re ported that he had captured 40.000 prisoners and 60 guns, besides enormous quantities of =muni- tion and stores. while he estimated the Russian killed and wounded at 00000. 13.—Ministerial appointments: Mr. Walter Low, as Chief Secretary for Ireland ; Mr. Gerald Balfour as President of the Local Government Board ; Lord Salisbury as President of the Board or. Trade; and the Don. Ailwvn Fellowes as President of the Beard of Agriculture. 17.—A painful in: 'ssion was made in St. Petersburg by the news that General J(uropatkiiu had been relieved of his functions as commander - in - chief and was succeeded by General Li ne- vite li, Neither event appears to have been ex- pected in the Ilus- ian capital, al- though it was well understood abroad that (lene- rid had asked to be 'relieved. The nomination of General Linevitch as his successor caused surprise everywhere. • 18.—Prince and Princess of Vales visited East Ham. Stepney L.C.C. election : Mr. A. 0. Good- rich (U.). 1777; Lord Denman (R.), 1375; Mr. A. A. \Vatts (Soc.), 111. Football: Scotland beat Ireland by 4 goals to 0 (A.) ; Scotland beat Ireland by 8 points to 0 (R.) 21.—Mukden—revised estimate of casualties: Russian, 175,000 including prisoners; Japanese, .50.000. 25.—Cross-Country: A. Aldridge won the In- ternaticnal Chamnionship.</p>
        <p>Z7.—Murder ana attempt murder at Deptford by masked men. Mr. Gerald ',oder. M.P.. ap- p.ointed a Lord of the Trea.sury and Junior Ministerial 'Whip.</p>
