        <head>JUNE.</head>
        <p>JUNE.</p>
        <p>I.—YorksliFre (Whitby): Mr. N. Burton (IL), 4547 ;Mr. G. Beckett (U.). 4102—majority, 445. [Mr. E. Beckett (U.) succeeded to peerage.] 3.—liussex (Chichester): Lord E. Talbot (U.), 4174; Mr. J. Allen (IL), 3762—majority, 412. Lord E. Talbot appointed a Junior Lord of the Treasury. G.—The Speaker anno, was loudly cheered when he rose from the chair, and in all quarters of the Honse the members uLcoyerod. Stating his reason for re- signing the Speaker. ship, be recalled the fact that in April he completed a term of 10 years' service RR Speaker. Up to then his health had been almost uninterrup- tedly good, but now, in consequence of recent illness, lie felt that he could not per- form his duties in a satisfactory manner.</p>
        <p>THE EX.SPEAKER.</p>
        <p>7.--Ehig of Spain entertained at luncheon at Guildhall. Norwegian Storthing deposed Ring Oscar.</p>
        <p>Altonso visited Windsor, dined, at Marlborough House, and attended State ball at Buckingham Eahate. 14.—Explosion on H.M.S. Magnificent: Four officers and 14 men injured, Lieutenant Stott:tit and four men fatally. Cork City: Mr. A. Hoehe (N.), unopposed; (Mr. J. F. N. O'Brien (N.) de- ceased). 15.—Wedding of Prince Gustavus Adolphus and Princess Margaret of Connaught at Vindsor. Donegal (N.): Mr. .J. Muldoon (N.) unopposed; (Mr. NV. O'Doherty (N.) deceased). 22.--Express train from New York to Chicago wrecked: 21 persons killed, 10 injured.</p>
        <p>26.—Prince- am] Princess Arisugawa of Japan arrived in London. being received by the Prince of Wales and the Duke of Connaught. 27.—King Oscar refused to agree to the offer of the Norwegian Crown to a Bernadotte Prince. South Malton-street shooting: Florence Doughty, at the Old Bailey, sentenced to seven years' penal Br:11'411de.</p>
        <p>20.—r:4st Finsbury : J. A. Baker (R.). 2320; N. L. Cohen (I:o,lsA2—majority, 768. (Mr. H. C. nic'.,ards (U.), deceased).</p>
