        <p>Roman Catholic ennrch ot Kits;lran by the parish priest, the Rev. P. Fagan, and his curate, the Rev. P. Murtagh. 7.—State Ball at Buckingham Palace. £3.—The King visited Lord and Lady Redesdale at Moreton-on-Marsh. 10.—West Down: Mr. H. Liddell (U.), 4037 i Mr. A. Beattie (1.11.). 3015—majority, 1022. 11.—Explosion in the pit of the United National Colliery Company at Wattstown,Glamorganshire', over 100 lives lost. 12.—Princess of Wales gave birth to a son. 13.--British officers visited Paris as the rests of the French Government.</p>
        <p>_ : Mr. F. W. Chance (K.), 3616 ; Mr. L. Sanderson, K.C. (U.), 2586; majority, 10.30, 15.—King and Queen opened new Home for Officers' Widows and Daughters at Wimbledon.</p>
        <p>19.—Duke of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha assumed the reins of Government of the Duchy. 2l.—Explosion on the U.S. gunboat Bennington at San Diego; about 270 men killed and injured.</p>
        <p>22.—The King was present at Risley when Armourer - Sergeant Comber, 2nd East Surrey, won the King's Prize; Ser- geant Souter won the Silver Medal; Colonel Sandeman, Gloucester Yeo- manry, won the St. George's Vase. 26.—The King un- veiled Royal Engi• neers' war memorial, and opened new Royal Naval Hos- pital at Chatham.</p>
        <p>27.-- Collision be- KING'S PRIZE WINNER. tween electric trains on the Lancashire and Yorkshire Railway, outside Liverpool; twenty lives lost. 28.--Pertnanent income of £50.000 a year ob- tained for the h:ing's Hospital Fund. 29.--At the Old Bailey, Arthur Devereux, was sentenced to death.</p>
        <p>31.--Cotton operatives decided to cease work unless an all•roond advance in wages of five per cent. was conceded.</p>
        <p>AUGUST.</p>
        <p>3.—lnfant son of the Prince and Princess of Wales christened at Sandringham, and received the names of John Charles Francis. s.—Death of Mr. A. Asher, M.P. 7.—French fleet arrived at Cowes; the King entertained the superior French officers, and conferred honours.</p>
        <p>IL---The King lunched with Admiral Caillard on the flagship Massdna. . . . .</p>
        <p>10.--L'Yeeneh officers entertained at lincheon at the Guildhall; mauniticent reception in London.</p>
        <p>11.—Parliament prorogued. French sailors entertained at the (luildriall while the officers visited Windsor, and afterwards dined with Mr. Bid rour.</p>
        <p>12.—French naval officers entertained by both Houses of Parliament in Westminster Hall, memorable speeches. 15.-I)evereux executed at Pentonville. 17.—Norway'ssecession: Overwhelming majority in its favour obtained.</p>
        <p>B.—lleath of Sir J. Haslett, M.P. (IL), Belfast (N.).</p>
        <p>20.—Lord Curzon resigned the Governor- Generalship of India. From a Par- liamentary paper it appeared that the Viceroy's action was primarily due to a difference of opinion with the Borne Government regArding the ap- poi ntineut of the first Military Sup- ply Member of the Council of India. 2l.—Miss Keller- man. and Messrs. New, Burgess and II attempts to swim the</p>
