        <head>OCTO BE R.</head>
        <p>OCTO BE R.</p>
        <p>3.—Opening of the Church Congress, Wey- mouth.</p>
        <p>o.—The King consented to.receire an address from the Poplar Borough Conned on the Unem- ployed question. Mr. E. 'l'. Salvesen, N.C.. ap- pointed to succeed Lord Kincairney as a Judge of Court of Session.</p>
        <p>D.—Marriage of the Duke of Saxe-Coburg- Gotha and Princess-Victoria Adelaide of Schles- wig - Holdtein - tionglerbarg - Gliieksburg. Earl Spencer seized with illness.</p>
        <p>l2.—Admirn-1 Sir Gerald Noel, with officers and men, arrived in Tokio; magnificent welcome.</p>
        <p>Sir Henry Irving expired suddenly at Brad- ford a quarter of an hour after he had finished a perform- ance of "Becket " at a local theatre; the announcement of the death of the great actor was received with universal sor- row, 14. Treaty of Peace signed hv the Emperors of Japan and Russia. Yorks. (Barkston Ash) an- nounced: J. Andrewa (R.) 4176; G. Lane- Fox (U.). 4h-1--ma- jority, 22.5. (Sir R. Gunter (U.). deceased). 16.--The King, acecinipaMed by the Queen. Prii.eess Victoria and the Primp and Princesa of Wales, laid the foundation stone of the new (iele'ral Post Ofliee. Death of Bishop Ellicott, for 43 Bishop of Gloucester.</p>
        <p>17.-Paris Municipal Councillors received by the King at Buckingham Palace. 18.-The King opened Kingsway : Brilliant scene. 19.-Prince and Princess of Wales left England for India. Mr. 13alfour presented with the Free- dom of Edinburgh. and Mr. John Morley and Mr. Carnegie with that of Montrose. 20.--Funeral of Sir Henry Irving in ‘Vestminster Abbey. 21.-Centenary of Nelson's death celebrated: World-wide honours. 25.-Announced thaZ the Ring was about to confer the Order of the Garter Tilton the Mikado, and that the British Legation at Tokio was to be raised to the rank of an Embassy. 26.-Hampstesul 4. S. Fleitol.sr 4225; G. F. Rowe (R.). -majority,422. (Mr. Milvain, K.C. (U.) appointed Jud g e Advocate Gen.n.x.l. General Booth pre- sented with the iT ee- doin of the City ; the General was reee:vtd with great acclama- tion by the crowds in the streets when on his way to the Guildhall, and his speech, in reply to the address, was listened to with sy GENERAL BOOTH. pathetic attention by a large and distinguished audience. NOVEM BER. 1.--British cruiser monad ron, under Prince Louis of Battenberg. an in ellesaiwke Itay. 2.-Earl and Countess of Miuto left England for India. Alessi's. Yarrow and Co. derided to remove their works from the Thames to the North. 3.-Mr. Chamberlain reopened his liscal cam- paign. 4.-Great massacre of Jews in all parts of Russia. especially at Odessa. Mr. J. Morley unveiled statue of Mr. Gladstone in the Strand. 0.-Death of Sir O. Williams, the founder of the Y.M.C.A. 9.-Birthday honours: Title of Princess Royal conferred on the llticliess of Fife, whose daughters are to bear the title Highness and be styled Princesses. Captain Wells resigned his position as principal agent of the Con -ervative party. 11.-The Queen issued an appeal to the nation for the unemployed, and forwarded a subscription of £2OOO. 13.-Prince of Wales laid foundation stone of new Alexander Dock at Bombay. King of the Hellenes and Prince and Princess Nicholas arrived at Windsor on a visit to the King and Queen. The Royal party drove to the castle through crow- ded and decorated streets in the car- riages. On alighting, King George was received by the officers of the House- hc4d and conducted to his splendid suite of apartments overlooking the north terrace. 14.-State baneuet at Windsor in honour of the King of the Hellenes. 15.-King of the Hollenes entertained by the City Corporation. 16 -The King injured his ankle while out shoot- ing. Queen's Unemployed Fund: The King gave a donation of 2000 guineas, and the Prince of Wales, 1000 guineas. 18.-Prince Charles of Denmark elected Ring of Norway, and adopted title muf Booker' VII." L. and S.W. Railway steamer wrecked off St. Malo ; 128 lives lost, only six of the passengers and crew being saved. 19.-Fire at a Glasgow lodging-house; 39 lives lost. 20.-Unemployed marched through London to Hyde-park. 23.-Sir H. Campbell-Bannerman on Home Rule, at Stirling. 24.-Itesignation rumours: Meeting of the Cabinet. The Queen and the King of the Hellenes left Buckingham Put lace for Sandringham. 25.-hing and Queen of Norway made their State entry into Christiania. Lord Rosebery, at Bodmin, pronounced against Borne Rule. 26.-News reevived of a great mutiny at Sebastopol. rebel am ilorm being joint 0 by troops. Gale routed the coast: Enormous damage. 27.-Sir Arthur Nicolson appoitited British Ambassador to St. Petersburg. Yorks (Nor- manton) : 3lr. F. Ball (11. and I,a h.), unopposed. Mr. Parrott (R. and Lat),). deeeased. 28 --Kaiser opened the Reichstag : Remarkable speech. 29.-Princess Royal opened Ring's labour tents. DEC EM BE'R. 1.-Prince of Wales received Imperial Service troops at Lahore. Imperial Defence Committee : Resignation of Lord R,d,erts. 4.-Resignation .of the U :onist Government presented to his , Majesty. Mr. lihi- fonr lißd so mull- ence of the Ring and tendered hie resignation a a Prime Al inister and First Lord of the Treaaur), which his Majesty was graciously pleaset to aceept. Mr. Justice Wills resigned. be i g succeualed by Mr. B. Sutton. Mr. J. Atkinson ap- pointed a Lord of ippmul, Air. . Cii mpb el , Solici- tor-General for Ireland. being appointed Attorney-General in his stead. 5.-hir 11. Campbell-Bannerman received by the King. accepted office as Prime Minister. Sir J. Fisher, First Sea Lord., promoted to Admiral of the Fleet. 6.-Hants (New Forest): Mr. H. Compton (Ca, 4533; Sir R. Hobart (R.). 4310-majority, 191 (Mr. J. Scott Montagu succeeded to peerage). 7.--Field-Marshal Oyama viade his entry into Tokio. 8.-Queen's Unemployed Fund amounted to £llB,OOO. 0.-I)eath of Sir Richard Jebb, M.P. 10.-The Meg approved of Sir Henry Campbell- Bannerman's Cabinet, and the list of appoint- ments was pub- lished. Sir Ed- wird Grey was appointed to the Foreign Office, and Mr. Asquith as Chancellor of aat Exchequer. Mr. John Burns no Mr. Lloyd- George attained Cabinet rank. 11.- Fir. Bah fonrand the other outgoing 31i ids- ters attended a Council at Buck- THE NEW PREMIER. ingham Palace, delivered up the seals of office, and took leave of his Majesty. At a second Council the new Minixters received the seals of office and took the oath, those of them who were not already Privy Councillors being sworn of the Privy Council.</p>
