      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>RESULTS OF SATURDAY'S GAMES.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T. P. Oldham Huns let Bradford 5 937 Naighley York Bull .... . _ __ .</p>
        <p>Warrington Wakefield T. ... 1 2 R Batley Brighouseß Wigan Leigh _</p>
        <p>Broupbton R. ... 4 10 28 Barrow Runcorn Salfotd Rochdale H.</p>
        <p>Played on the gronnd of the club marked *.</p>
        <p>MONDAY'S 3fATCHES. G. T. P. G. T. r. Leigh Ifunlet 4 2 14 Halifax Bradford Oldham , 2 7 25 Rochdale D'nets 0 1 3 Bionghton Wgers 0 3 11 Salford York Wakefield St. Helens Hull Kingston ... 2 1 7 Wigan 1 0 2 Pontefract</p>
        <p>RUGBY UNION.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE couyry .ICrNIORS v. CHESHIRE</p>
        <p>COUNTY JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>At Rochdale, before a good attendance, Cheshire had the best of the first half, and Bickerston scored a try and dropped a goal. in the second half Lincashire played better, and Snith and Tracey crossed the line the goal kicks failing. Ferguson (Stockton Heath) scored two tries for Cheshire, and Johnson sdded a . third. Result :—Cheshire 2 goals 4 tries, Lancashire 2 tries. STOCKTON E E Ifni V. ECC L ES. —This match was ! pled at Stockton Heath on Saturday and resulted in an easy win for the visitors. There was a fair attendance of spectators and Eccles started against the wind. Play had only been in progress a few minutes when the visitors' forwards crossed the line as the result of a combined rush. This performance they repeated a few minutes later. Stockton Heath next assumed the aggressive and from some loose play under the posts the hall was kicked to the wing vhere Bouchier scored. Eccles again took up the attack and after some splendid combination a third try was added. The home team once more asserted themselves and forced play to the visitors' half where the ball travelled to Boucher who again scored at the corner. From this stage Eccles did prac- tically all the scoring thou Daintith got over for the " Heathens," and the final score in the visitors favour was 29 points to 9 points.</p>
        <p>• • r•iTOCKTONT HEATH V. ECCI.F.S " A."—On account of the junior county match at Rochdale, Stockton Heath took only a weak team to Eccles on Tuesday to meet the Eccles second contingent. The home team was at full strength and were more than a match for the " Heathens " winning by 19 points to 3 points. A Cornei was the scorer for the visitors, that player dropping a goal from heyond the half way mark.</p>
        <p>The High School. The other evening at the Co-operative Hall, Sankey- street. the Mayoress (Mrs. Bolton) very kindly dis- tributed the certificates to the pupils of the Misses Partington. Referring to the work done during the 12 months ending in July, 31 candidates had been entered for' the various examinations, all of whom had been successful, there were 11 for the Orford Locals, viz.: —M. Forshaw, E. Partington, F. Warburton, A. Rennie, R. Dunn, (juniors). A. Kenyon, D. Percival, D. Bracer:amp. F. Bennet, E, Broome, G. Wilson (Preliminaries). Ten for the Incorporated Society of Musicians in pianoforte viz.: —M. -Jolley (Grace, 111.), D. BracecampM. Forshaw, L. Percival (Grade I.). Cr. Whitlow (hononrs), D. Bennie, E. Howard, N. Warbnrton, N. Fowles, D, Row., (Preparatory Grade). Two in violin viz. : —E. Partington (Grade II.), A. 'Kenyon (Grade T.) Eight for the College of Preceptors viz.: —D. Brace- r:amp. A. Highnett, _L. Percival, A. Kenyon, F. Onniston, Cr. Wilson, E. Bro be, F. Bennett. A few weeks ago, a prize was offered, by a friend of the school for the be-t essay oa Lord Nelson, the successful competater being Emily Broome. During the term recently ended, E. Partington has passed the Higher Division School Examinations of the Associ- ated Board of Royal Academy and Royal College of N'lnsic. The following pupils have also been success- ful at the recent examination held by the Incorporated Society of Musicians: D. Bennie, Grade 1 (Pass); A. Horton (honours), D. Percival (honours) Prepara- tory Grade. The distribution of certificates was , followed by a most successful entertainment which was greatly appreciated. In the first part of the I programme, the youttger children rendered a very pretty action song called " Tambourines " also a charming floral drill. Miss E.Partington was warmly I applauded for her violin solo. A humorous recitation I by Miss Alice Partington was enthusiastically received The feature of the evening was a play called " A Royal Rose of Merrie England." The young per-1 , formers looked very charming in their fancy costumes, and their rendering of the various characters nag most I creditable. The fortunes of the Princess Henrietta I (Miss Marjorie Bennet) aroused great interest, hnd tier cavalier Mends (the Misses E. Partingtoa, A. Kenyon, D. Bracecampl. cooniveil at her escape and tricked the Roundhead Soldiers (the Misses E.Broome, ; G. Wilson, and C. Deas) in a masterly manner. Great I amusement was caused by the arguments between the I nurse ()fiss L. Dennett) and the Paritan housekeeper I (Miss L. Percival.) Miss M. Forshaw, as the King's I jester, created roars of laughter, especially when she nersmated the bear. The Misses Gladys Whitlow and Katie Groc ,tt as cavalier children were vary charm- ing. Several pretty fancy dances were introduced in the play. and were performad 'most grasefnlly. Dnring the Christmas venation time Misses Partington are removing their school t 1 Stlektoe Lodge, Stockton Heath, where they will have increased accommodation.</p>
        <p>TIMM' FFI/111 A T.ocAt. WouKs.— 114 n CLIARkrfER At the Warrington police court on Tuesday. Edward ItOsP and Crown-street, was charged with steal- ing 40Ih. of brass steps of the value of 40s.,the property of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and iron Company Limited —Mr. F. G. Taylor (Messrs. Greenall and CO:) proseentoi and stated that a watchman was making his round of the works on Saturday night when he heard a noise. Turning his light in the direction of a fitting shop he saw the prisoner hiding. He had a number of brass steps in his possession and on being asked what he was doing he said " Christmas time, I live had a drop of drink." Prisoner had entered the fitting shop through an aperture in the corrugated iron root'. Mr. Taylor pressed for a severe penalty as thefts from local works were frequent. P.C.Bennett said he saw the prison- er on Satnrdav night end he was under the influence of drink—Prisoner told the Bench he wee " helpless" and did not know how the brass came into his nossessinn.—The Chief Constable said Since got his living principally by theft find , vagrancy. He had been before the Court 40 times, lie had often been convicted of theft and had served Nix months for an aggravated nsrtalt on his poor old, mother on whom he sponged for is livelihood. He NV:l4g one of the worst characters in the town.—Prisoner was sentenced to three months' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>TUESDAY'S MATCHES.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>Dunslet 3 3 13 Bradford .. 0 0 0 York 3 3 13 Normonton Salford *Leeds Borrow Batley lituldi4rsfleld iinlitax 2 1 7 Broughton R. 0 1 S Wakefield I'. Wigan Keieblev Hull Kingston H. 1 I 5 Bull *Abandoned owing to fog.</p>
