      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Warrington Overwhelm St. Helens.</head>
        <p>Warrington Overwhelm St. Helens.</p>
        <p>St. Helens were the visitors to Warrington on S ttur- day and a vigorous game WILS Oa result. The visitors mere particularly active in the fir.t. half. but deliberate obstruction, thrice got them penalised Fish kicking two goals and Preston one. A fine piece of play by Creevev was backed up by the forwards,and Briers was imlacky in being pulled down almost under the posts. f-time Wnrrington, 3 goals, (ti points) St.. Helens, nil. Turning round the play for a long time savoured of a scrappy description, and had it not been for the clever display of Hockenhall the game would have fallen very fiat. Tt was eventually r,icd almost in a moment from mediocre state to a period of brilliance. for Fish taking a wide pass dodged his way over amidst a storm of cheering. Preston landed the goal, and though St. Helena played up pluckily, they were only plodders at the finish, for an effort by the forwards culminated in Jordon scoring and Fiala kicking the goal the result being Warrington, 5 goals 2 tries (16 points) ; St. Helens, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Warrington Lose at Leigh.</head>
        <p>Warrington Lose at Leigh.</p>
        <p>There were hopes that, Warrington would show some of their best, form on Monday at Leigh, but they had to play Rec And fiddle. tholigh they came near scoring on several occasions. A gate of 5,000 assembled. War- rington played Lloyd at half in preference to Brooks. Leigh went off with a tremendous dish, and a penalty goal kicked by Dunbavin eventually rewarded their , efforts. Then Warrington had one splendid chance, which was spoiled through a forward pass to Fish. The forwards were finely matched, and it was a great treat to watch the rushing and counter rushing, varied by quick passing by the backs. Bennett. the Leigh centre, retired hurt, het the Leigh forwards held their ours so well that Leigh crossed over with n two points load. Bennett resumed on the restart, rtod plow became more exciting than ever. Warrington repeatedly swept. down towards the Leigh line, only to he repulsed, Clarkson being in great form. Then TAPlzh's torn came, and the forwards gradually gained the tipper hand. Neville had two fine chances, but lost, them through hesitation. Ultimately, after a splendid run by Bennett, Leigh got near the Warrington line. and a bit of trickery hr the Leigh halves reenited in Whittaker getting in behind the posts, Dunhavin gosling. Result :—Leigh, 2 goals and 1 try (7 points), Warrington nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>Salford's Lucky Win.</head>
        <p>Salford's Lucky Win.</p>
        <p>There WAR a fine crowd at Warrington on Tuesday for the Salford match. fully 6,000 people being present Warrington's centre positions were still uncertain, and Brooks and Fenyon replaced Jordan and Jones. S Iford had Rees on the wing in place of Bell. Fine weather again prevailed and a lively cenrest was witnessed. Salford had the advantage of a slight breeze during, the first hair, and Lomas, Preston, and Thomas utilising touch well, Salford obtained a footing inside the home (matters. Fish, Kenyon, and finclrenhall excelled in defensive work. awl eventually Fish ran smartly along touch and forced Salford back. S dfor4 then pressed, and after Johns had been held up on the line, a. hid blander by Knight led np to flees scoring near the posts, for Lolllfts to kick a goal. Warrington rallied After this, hot Fish and Kenyon were held inches inside. Widdeson checked Johns after a brilliant rum, and though Warrington again got down, at half-time Salford led by 5 points to nil. Tinning round, Warrington forncd the nac-.. end mnde 'mid bids for a, score, only to find Salford huillient on the defence. Preston was penalised,and Fish placed a goal. Bursts by Lomas and Preston warte instru- mental in taking the game to Knight's charge hut another fine clearance was followed by strong forward work. Salford were penalised. end Fish again kicked a goal. Exciting play was witnessed at midfield. and wheeling the pack the Warrington forwards took the tilll over at the corner. Lonvas, however, got up in time to kick the ball dead. Then Wildeson had hard lines in not. scoring from a c,ross-kick. Warrington vreFsed hotly in the concluding stages, and Fish on one occasion get dear, but failed to pass the Salford custodian. Resultr—Salford 1 goal and 1 try(s p Warrington 2 goals (4 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>Hunslet v. Warrington.</head>
        <p>Hunslet v. Warrington.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday the Wilderspoil brigade atte anted a well-nigh hopeless task when they tackled the York- shire Cup-holders at Park Side. thinslet are a very powerful side this season and easily vanquished War- rington's forces. The weather was excellent and 8,000 people ,ssembled to witness this, the last of the Christmas holiday engagements. Teams : llunslet—Place, back ; Smith, Eagers. W. Goldthorpe, and W. Ward, three-quarter backs : Everson and A. E. Goldthorpe, half backs ; Wilson, Jnkes,Brookes, Walsh, Shooter, Wray, and Glew. forwards. IVarrington—Knight, back ; Fish, Brooks. .Tones, and Widdeson, three-quarter tacks; Ilockenhall and Lloyd, half hacks ; Preston. Boardman, Shugars,Lunt, Thomas, Swift. Harmer, and Jolley. forwards. Referee, Mr. W. McCutcheon, Oldham. Baying lost the toss, Albert Goldthorpe commenced the game, and straight away Hunslet attacked strongly. Everom was then the initiator of a good movement by bursting past his opposing halves. On facing Brooks he vassed wide out to Eagers, and the latter transferred to Ward, who was forced into touch a few yards outside the line. Warrington were then penalised for off-side play, and from an easy position Albert Golthorpe lowered the goal. After an interesting duel in touch kicking between the opposing helves, Albert Gold- thorpe opened out the game, and the hall going to Eagers he sent it to Walter Goldthorpe, The last-named sprinted in fine style until near the line, and then gave the ball to Smith, who romped over. From ilea.. touch Albert Goldthorpe placed a fine goal. Shortly before half-time Smith again scored after fine combina- tion between Everson and Walter Goldthorpe. This time Albert Goldthorpe failed at goal, and fin:islet crossed over leading by 2 goals and 2 tries (10 points) to nil. The game in the first half had been very fast, end on resuming it was obvious that both sides were tiring, there being a lack of slim, and dash in the methods of both teatas.So far as the play wentAVarrington did most of theattacking,and it was only sound defence that kept the Lancastrians out. The scene was changed in quite thematic fashion. Engers broke away on the right and on fscing Knight kicked across. Ward came up at, full speed, gathered the ball, end ran to within a few yards of the line. Here he passed to Wilson, and the Ilunslet forward scored a try. This was a fine movement, and thoroughly deserved the score. Albert Goldthorpe failed at goal. This occured about a quarter of an hour from the close, and though Warrington tried hard to reduce the lead they were unsuccessful. Ihmslet there- fore ran out winners for the fourth time within five dsys by a score of 2 goals and 3 tries (13 points) to nil.</p>
        <p>WARR INGToN "A "V. Lot u " A."—A large crowd of spectators assembled on the Wilderspool gronnel on Monday morning when Warrington " A met and easily cl.feated the Leigh recond contingent. From the start ' the home team held the upper hand and tries were scored by Ken 3on (3), Harmer f), and Brooks (1), against. one try terorded by the Visitors, the final score being Warrington 2 gools 5 tries, Lei gh 1 try.</p>
      </div>
