      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>DlieliMßEß 30th 1905.</p>
        <p>The Tariff Rdformers are ELOQUENT making Moan about the -CANVASSERS. dishonesty of their 4ntagno-</p>
        <p>fists. One of them com- plains of weird misrepresentation, the suppression of contexts, the nistortion of pronouncements, the perversion of facts, the misapplication of statistics, the exaggeration of emphasis, the misquotation of set phrase,—and so on, and so on. This is scarcely the sort of grievance that we should expect the Tariff Reformers to feel very keenly. They cer- tainly ought to have been hardened against it by the character of the leaflet literature which they have been spreading abroad from Bir- mingham. When we come to consider the matter more closely, however, it is merely in the nature of things that they should raise an outcry on the alleged ground that the friends of Free Trade are not stating their case fairly. If the battle for Protection is ever to be woo, it will have to be won by effrontery, and as the average elector can hardly be expected to examine all or even a comparatively small portion of what can be said on both sides,bold but apparently pathetic assevera- tions that the Free Traders are not speaking the truth may in such a case be regarded as " good business."</p>
        <p>Care should be taken; however, that whilst accusing the enemy of bad faith, one does not on one's own side make assertions, the truth of which cannot only be immediately disproved, but are in themselves of a comic character. We will illus- trate what we mean by an instance. Mr. OHIOZZ.A. MONEY, who has a tine grasp of the Free Trade problem, and is a candidate for one of the London constituencies, is assailed because, as it is stated he was a year ago an alien. This we believe is not the case. But we will let the statement 'pass. Mr. Curozza-MoNny would in that case balance Mr. Louis Stricnsia, who, not long be- fore he was returned to Parliament on the Coniorvative side, was in a similar predicament. But the point to which we wish to draw attention is that it is further alleged against Mr. ClliozzA. MoxEr that he is employing German canvassers, with a very imperfect knowledge of the language, to solicit votes. Of course Mr. CurozzA-MoNsir cannot employ canvassers, either German or English. Did he do so he would be ousted from the seat even if he were successful at the poll. Nor would he employ German canvassers in any case. It may be surmised. that if he were an alien, he would be an Italian, of course, and highly unlikely to employ Germans to help him in his election. But there is a good deal more in this accusation than meats the eye. There is even a certain amount of whimsical truth in it.</p>
        <p>The fact is that Mr. Curozza.-MoNny is being assisted in his eleaion by German sausages They are horseineat sausages, and they have been brought over to this country for the purpose of showing what kind of diet it is on which the work- ing classes of Germany are to a large extent fed. A similar demonstration was made by Sir ALFRED HanmswonTn before be became a Protectionist or a Peer, or even a Baronet. He sent over to Germany for a quantity of black bread, of which be offered a free distribution for the purpose of showing what bitter bread is that of a Pro- tectionist country. Unfortunately for himself, it is not now possible for him to make a similar demonstration on the other side. That is by the way, however. The point is that Mr. Cuiezza-Morrav's German sausages were seized by the Sanitary Inspector and taken before Mr. PLOWDEN at the Marylebone Police Court. There was some very rseful explication of the case in consequence, Mr. PLowDEN• ultimately ordering the sausages to be given up to their owner, and saying that he had no disposition to interfors with Mr. CnrozzA-MoNav's electoral campaign. And this is just how Germans who speak indifferent English are being employed in the Marylebone contest. The language spoken by these particular "canvassers" is quite explicitly German. It is understood to be an odour not very distinctly inferior to that of Limburger cheese.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
