      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <p>The "Daily News" has LIBERAL AND been expressing its cond. LABOUR. deuce in the return of the Progressive party with an ample working majority over both Tories and Irish. The only source of danger that this journal perceives is in " unhappy divisions in the Progres- sive ranks," and it adds :—" It is not our purpose here to discuss the question whether Lheralistn is an adequate instrument for the aims of L tbaur. Toe point we wish to make is that for the immediate present at all events the Government of Sir H. Campbell-Bannerman is the only weapon at the command of L about and it is only by 'sharpening and strengthening that weapon that Labour can recover the corporate rights of which the Law Courts have robbed it and which the Tory G wernment refused to restore. Nor is this all. Labour stands for Free Trade, for anti-militarism, for the unlocking of the land to the people, for a drastic treatment of the =employe I problem, for the principle of popular control of edu :ation and the liquor traffic. On these and other p )ints it is in entire symnathy with the programme laid down by the Peirne Minister at the Albert Hall and upon its expression of that sympathy at the ballot box will largely depend the power of the Government to carry that programme in the teeth of a powerful and unscrupulous Opposition and a wholly 51=10lIss of L )rds. We have no doubt as to thel sympathy of the Labour members who will be returned to Parliament. We know that, what- ever Lioerals may be found wanting, they will al ways be discovered in the right lobby. There have been no more consistent supporters of the , Pc.4gressive cause—call it Liberal, Radical or socialist—in the present Parliament than those who stand in the name of the 1.43.1f1ur Representation Committee, ar.d we hope to 860 their numbers greatly reinforced next month." We hope these outEmoken and sensible remarks by one of the most democratic organs in the eonntry will receive fall attention in Warrington. Time was when the local Labour group professed to class Liberals and Tories together as the •:,sornmon enemy, but the claims of reason and expediency have modified their attitude, and we believe the great majority of Labour men in Warrington are prepared to vote at the next election on the lines of the policy sketched by the " D aily News." At this present juncture, when there is a crisis in national policy, and when there is a direct representative of Lab iur in the Cabinet, ,no Labour man worth his salt will waste time •solitting hairs, but throw himself nnreservelly On the side of the Liberals in the fight between Monopoly and the People.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Daring the Christmas holi- PREPARING Fon days there has naturally THE FIGHT, been a lull in political work, but the campaign will begin in earnest next week when several meetings will he hold. The Liberals of NVarrington are buckling on their armour with the determination to secure a great triamph for Free Trade and progress. Much educational work, which is bound to bear -fruit, has already been done in the constituency hut what is of paramount importance is that every class of the electorate—and more especially those who do not in the ordin try course devote them- selves with any serionsnets to national questions —should be thoroughly impressed with the magnitude of the issues involved. To this end the heln is needed of those who are anxious that Eugland should not depart from that policy which lies at the root of her corn- ,rnercial prosperity, and the call to arms is meeting whh an enthusiastic response. The main issue is a simple one, and it is succinctly put by Mr. Jpseph Arch, an ex-M.P., and president of the National Agricultural Labourers' Union, who has had experience of the old days of Protection, He says : r Every vote given Balfour, Chamber- :Mu, and Co. means dear food and less of it ; decreased employment and higher taxation •, depression of the funds and a huge system of gambling by secret syndicates with your food. I solemnly declare to you that the permanent general misery and stagnation of trade in the host times of Protection were infinitely greater than anything the country has had to sufhr Li the severest depressi ms which have occasionally occurred under Free Trade. Are you willing to tax your daily food, clothing, and all the neces- saries of life and impose an unbearable load of .financial burclocs on your children and children's children P That is the great issue in your hands. Co the question put by Mr. Arch, Warrington, we are convinced, will return an emphatic nega- tive.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>In his reply to Mr. A. H. MR. F. MONKS Crosfield, which wo pnhlish AND FREE TRADE. in another coloron, Mr. F. Monks affirms' himself "a</p>
        <p>thorongh Free Trader," and then proceeds to use ' a numlnr of Protectionist arguments in support of his assertion. We can only presume that the , -remnants of his Liberalism are responsible for the little fiction, about heing a Free Trader, in which he hidalgos. Mr. Monks goes on to say that he mrplerstands Free Trade to mean "free to exports , as well as to imp nts." Our contention is that iinder the existing system we are perfectly free both to import and to export. Unfortunately we cannot make laws for other nations, and if they themselves are not wise enough to let their people obtain goods cheaply that is scarcely a reason why our G wernreent should compel us to pay dearly. The foreigner's " wall of Protection" is more against his own people—who have to pay the consequent increased prices—than against us. No nation is able to exclude our products; we send goods to all parts of the world despite the wall of tariffs and it is our Free Trade policy which enables us to obtain oar raw materials •an cheaply. In what protected countries except America—where the cost of living is excessively high —does the worker get better wages and cheaper food than in E 'gland ? This is a question which naturally arises from Mr. Monks's argu., merits, and one we should like him to answer. Can .he tell us, too, where and when the workingman ,expressed himself content to pay the extra cast of diving ? With regard to the labourer. We think it is beyond argument that he leaves the land 'because of our hoary and despotic laws which have made the land the property of the fe w and rendered 'it impossible for the many to sabsist on its fruits, as is their birthright. We might remind our correspondent that the Liberal p arty have never refused to " consider the fiscal question on its merits." On the contrary they have shown considerable eagerness to fight the battle forensically in the House of Commons, but the Tory party always ran away because, as Mr. Balfour explained, they could not be trusted to argue the question. Mr. Monks says it is easier to complain of increased taxation than to reduce It and he doubts whether any sane man expects the New Goverment to do any better than the late one in its financial alminiatration. At the risk of making a trite observation we are bound to say that it certainly cannot do worse. The late Government nearly doubled our expenditure, increased our taxation, ran up our National debt, and dissipated the surplus left by the late Liberal Government and the large amount of molev they 'received from Sir William flarcmirt's death duties. With regard to Colonial preference it will be time to consider the matter when the colonies ask for such a concession. Mr. Balfour has told as that, so 'far, there is no such demand fro n that anarter.</p>
        <p>Lt rirtrash Immo.; NvAKa Ur., Amcnica. -"Ohm been schooled for so long into the belief that Drikish trade abroad is slowly but surely being filched away by those terrible Germans, and those equally terrible, hustling Americans, that it is quite a surprise to 'and that America, at any rate, is not by any means so greatly convinced of the security ?1 her position as a rival of this country In foreign markets. An American Consul- General, who ought to know something, is. Irn fact, telling his countrymen to "wake up," _Cher° is something reminiscent in the injunction ; but it is not the one we are accustomed to hear in connection with things American. Have we not always been given to -understand (by the Amen- it is true, but, then, like 111 r. Gilbert's renchman, they "ought to know,", that</p>
        <p>1 3 1 .. . . . _ s' • ~ t, • ~ ' 4,';'t' -i.-:-. ,r ..„!;„. ~....::: , 1 f • a 1 Ii C V U 13 .Er it 13 IT it it rE Pi th th CI bI M 10 to it CI on Tis to lat Inc an, G 3 Too Wi 'to ( auc 'far, , beli Fur( Gl4r Am, Ind any of in 'Gen in f Thai but coin alwa cans 'firer 1,- ~.. 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <p>our Transatlantic friends are "about the widest awake on earth ?" British Consuls have been accustomed to reproach the British manufacturer for failing to adapt his method or his goods to foreign requirements. Our American friend, however, tells hts country- men that the British trader is a model in the matter of obligino• and suiting his customers which it will pay to follow. By-and-bye, says the candid Yankee, American production will overbalance the amount required for home con- sumption, and then the foreign market will have to be more sedulously cultivated than it is at pre- sent. It may be some consolation, after all the abus3 that the British trader has had to put up with, to find that he is feared in America if he is not appreciated at home. *</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Dr. Guinness Rogers e,elebrates his 83rd birthday to-day (Friday). Don't vote for the tutu who employs nobody, and who has no stake in the town ! * * The Tories trot'el out the Home Pale bogey again on Wednesday, but they frightened nobody but them- selves.</p>
        <p>* * Llovd-G orge is varying his electoral campaign in Carnarvon Boroughs with daily golf on the Criccieth links,which adjoin his native village of Llanystumdwy.</p>
        <p>0 ving to the holidays the Town CoUncil meeting whi3h in the ordinary course would be held next Tuesday evening has been postponed until the follow- ing week. * * Toon) were only four "drunks" for trial at the Polioe Court on Tuesday and the Chief Constable informed the Bench that the town had been quieter than usual this Christmas. Is IVarrington becoming more sober?</p>
        <p>* * • The most ardent -Radical or Socialist would hesitate to urge that children should be fed by the ratepayers, witi the ultimate aim of moistening the throats of dis`touest, and unpatriotic rearers of families.— " D Lily Graphic " * * * "In this Free Trade colutry we pay better wages to oar workmen. the hours of labour are sic wter; food is cheaper,- raiment is cheaper, the shelter of our ho 119 e is cheaper, there is greater freedom for the workingman than in any Protectionist cmatry in Earope."—Mr. Lloyd George at Bangor. *s* Consept.nt on the investizatiens mule in South If inc by Itr. Commissioner Reeve, the War Office art, it is stated, abent institute criminal proceed- in ts against certain army men in connection with the War Stores S:andal. Tali is another slap for the late administration which was afraid t 3 take the only ho test course open to it. *s* "or all sound of all bells —(bells, the music nighest I)lr(lering up in heaven) —most solemn and toucning is the pc ‘vhich rings out the Old Yea I never h) Lr it without a !gad) )ring-up of my Inin•l to a con- centration of all the images that have been diffused over the past twelvemonth ; all I have done or sn't )red. perform ul or neglected —in tint regretted ti tie. .11 begirt to know its worth, as when a person dies."--Charles. Lamb in "Essays of Eli 3."</p>
        <p>* * * Both the Warrington candidates are in the field,and tin voters will Roan have to det-,-itle between the " whole-hogger " • who w mid increase the price of food, and the Frea Trader who bases his fiscal beliefs o tha palicy which It ts mule the English the most prosparotts, the b .st-fed, and the best-housei nation in tin world. The " big loaf " is at present a substantial S disfying reality, but the "little loaf " will assuredly tire its place if ever we ara governed under a Protec- tioaist regime. _ . .</p>
        <p>Mr. John White must be an incurable and deter- mined optimist or he would never predict Mr. R. Pierpint's return by " a lar.ger majority than before," as he did on Wednesday •evening. The next speaker slid the majority would be reduced, which was rather disrespeAful to the Chairman of the Conservative A.ssooiation. Tie ought to know best surely? We believe thero will be n ) majority for Mr. Pierpoint at all, arid we dare wager something substantial in the wty of new hats that we are nearer the mark than Mr. White. . .</p>
        <p>* 5 * A. tribute to the strength of the new Cabinet is forthcoming from Calla Scott Holland, of St. Paul's Cathedral, who preached at Warrington Parish Church a few years age. Writing in " The Common- wealth " he says : :has become perfectly clear, even to our slow-witted friend, the man in the street, that we are in possession of a Cabinet of exceptional strength. Tim pLacing of the men at their befitting jobs has beau admirably m tnagal. The material to In usel is excellent in qu laity aad calibre. There is not a weak spot." * * After Mr. R. Pierpoint had been adopted as Conservative candidate on Wednesday he expressed the desire to see carried out between the two parties " a policy of friendliness and courtesy which he hoped would not 1.” infringed by the C amen-laves or those who honourably opposed them." We can all say " hear, hear" with fervour. But the policy has been alrotdy " infringed," And the Conservatives have not yet disavowed the insulting and cowardly allusions to Miss Crosfield,which were made from the platform of their first public meeting. And since then they have descended to cheap sneers at "foreign labour." There is apparently no connection between the words and deeds of some people. 55* Some good if rather grim stories of " judges' with are told in the current "Cornhill " by Visconnt St. Cyrus, and still better ones of their eccentricities, especially those of the Scotch judges. There is the felicitous confusion, for instance in Lord Swinton's mind, which was so unhinged by the French Revolution that he regretted he could not order Scotch Radicals to he thrown to the beasts, as Radicals were in the good old days of Imperial Rome. Still worse was Lord Braxfield (idealised as the Justine-Clerk in " Weir of Hanniston "), who was so mach amased when a Radical reminded him that even the foander of Christianity had been a reformer. • "Mach to be made of that," chuckled the irreverent Tory on the bench; "he was hanget." Lord Hermistoa was evidently amm who was amiable in his cups, for he once burst out in open court with, " CF o 1 Gad, my lairds, if the prisoner murdered his friend when drunk, what would he do when he was sober! "—a piquant inversion of oar modern notion of the effect Of drunkenness on criminal impulses. On the whole Viscount St. Cyres's stories give one no desire to erase "the judicial humourist" from Koko's " little list." •</p>
        <p>* * " Think of the most unlikely name you cm imagine and I'll show you it in the London Directory." The I man who set this disconcerting question to me was I not to be nut off his point. Lie had, it turned out, I spent his Christmas at the club mainly in company I with the new Directory. It is as difilmlt when called I upon to think of the preposterous as it is to think of some wise thing, but the season sugge3ted the name " Crusixs." " Right," said he, " Crasoe, George," and ; he showed it to me in the book. " Well, Gulliver, 1 i then." ," Five," he replied. I could say no more, • bat this give him the opportunity to say that there were four Deoths, twelve Coffins (live of .whom were cabi netmake rs).one Grave, and sixteen William Baileys. " You should have tried Dickens's characters," he said with a grin. "Thera is no Pick‘vick, no Copp3r- field. no Tnryam, no Snxlgrass„no Nickleby, no Gamp." " Well I mutt be getting, o 1," I said," to leave youto your literary labonrs." You'll find the names of all the great literary labourers still persist amongst us." h 3 went on. "There are two Tennysons, thirty-one Brownings, three Thackerays, ten Dickenses, eleven Milton; seven Shaksperos —three of them are Williams!" " Good night," I cried. " And a whole column of Shaws," he shouted after ins. " Are there any Asses ?" I could not refrain from shouting back. -" Yes," he roared triump'rautty. " But "—this with a clurkle —" ther3'i only 0143 A. 34 in [Andra." I did not correct him. —" Manchester Guardian."</p>
        <p>Notice. The Warrington " Examiner" is now published on Friday afternoon, price one penny, and is on sale at the Yarioas newsagents at about 4 o'cbck. Should any difficulty be experienced in getting the paper the proprietors will undertake to d -liver a copy at any ddress in Warrington by 5 o'clock on Friday at ter- no on Without extra charge. Address all commnnici, tions W" Examiner Office," Banrsey-strect Warrington</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events The annual Old Boys' dinner in connection with the Boteler Grammar School will be held at the Lion Hotel on Saturday January 13th.</p>
        <p>A watch-night service will be emilneted by the Bev. H. Dunnico at the Golborne-street, Baptist Church at 11.15 on Sunday night. The atihual congregational tea will be held on Monday evening.</p>
        <p>Special sermons will be preached at Wycliffe Church on Smithy by the Rev. John Yonge. At 11.15 p.m a watchnight service will be held. The annual old folk's treat will be held in the Parr Hall on Thursday next. At noon the old men will p irtake or dinner and at six in the evening tea will be served to the aged w.unm. A public entertainment will follow.</p>
        <p>At the penny porydar c )ncert on Saturday evening an excellent bioscopic entertainment will be presented Musical Successes. At the recent examination in connection with Trinity College of Music, London, Bessie Lee and Mary Dorothy Hall, pupils of the Misses Wiliam, The Priory School, were successful in passing the intermediate division (pianoforte). —At the reeent examination of the Victoria College of Haile, London Miss Mande M. Sloane passed in pianoforte playing 90 marks with honours. lAt the recent Examination held by the Incorporated Society of Musicians in pianoforte playing Miss Ethel Wilkinson gained 90 marks (honours) and Miss Alice Harding 92 marks (honours). The above are pupils of Mr. C. H. Dawson, 28, Arthur-street, Warrington. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC takes away that tired feeling.—(Advt.) Galvanized Roofing Sheets, Roofing Felts, ete. • Large stock.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge st., Warring ton.—Advt. Labour Bureau Opened. A Labour Bureau has been opened by the Distress Committee at the old School Attendance Ofaceq, Bank House, but so far very little MO has been made of it. Only six names had been registered up to noon to-day (Friday). The bureau is open each day from 9 ti 12 for male applicants for work and from 2 to 3 for females. Employers are asked to avail themselves of the bureau when they have places to fill, as tho character of each applicant is carefully inquired into. Mr. J. Fargher has been appointed secretary to the committee. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt. PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST</p>
        <p>_ • BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Warrington Eisteddfod. The pincipal attraction in Warrington sou New Years' Day will be the annual eisteddfod which • will be held in the Parr Hall. Since its inception the musical festival has been a groat success and Monday's proceedings pro:nise to eclipae all previous records. The number of comp st:tors has ineressal and whilst the old faturei have ben retained a now competition for humorous Lancashire dialect recitations has been introduced. Mr. Etnlyn D ivies, who will act in con- junction with \fr. J. Whewall (Stoke), as musical adjudicator, will give solos at tho proce sdings both in the afternoon and evening. The Rev. W. Wynn Davies will again act as conductor. Particulars as to prices &amp;c., will be found in our advertising columns. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER TILE CLOCK FOR BEST . BOOTS IN LINCASHIRE.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Petroleum Oil in barrels and drums delivered free.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, bridge et., Warring- ton.—Advt. Poirilar Penny Concerts. The Saturday night concerts which are held in Bold-street Wesleyan school continue to be well patronized and on Saturday evening there was again a crowded audience. Miss 13 Ale, from London, an elocutionist of considerable power contributed a num- ber of items for which she was encored. Mr. J, Hill (bass) pleased the audi”ne.o with his songs, Mr. Whittaker (concertina) and Mr: E. Smith also rec,-4i-ed applause, while Miss Winnie Jones gave two songs in excellent style. The humorous element was well sus- tained by Nfr. Scarisbrick. Miss Sterling acted as a ccompanist. ASK for CITLINDIG RAM'S lIIGH-CLASb MINERAL WATERS.—Advt. Iron Fencing of every description. Lowest prices.—J. S. GARLAND, Si to 55, Bridge st., Warr ington.—Advt. Ragged School and Mission. On Tuesday last the annual Christmas tea party 'to the poor children took place. About 430 sat down Ito a substantial meal consisting of cakes, currant and Iseed bread, and bread and butter. After tea a short concert was held, songs being rendered by Mrs. Gough, I Misses Titchard and Berry, recitations by Mrs. Barlow, Miss Healey and Mr. H. Wright, at the close of which the presents °lf the tree ware distributed and each child received an orange. On Thnrsday evening about 50 poor widows and 89 children were enter- tained to te t. The committee desire to tender their sincere thanks to all those who gave food, clothing and decorations which helped to make these treats so great a success. - _</p>
        <p>I - New Year's Gifts. Splendid New Stock.-4. S. I GAALSND, 51 to .15, Bridge st.,44rrington.—Advt. I 13e-to-date Fire Places.—l. S. GARLISD, 51 to 55,, 1 Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. . 1 The Priory School. ' Owing to th 3 roc tut beraa,,em mt of the Principals ' (Th) Misses Williams) no poblic.prize-giving or con- ! curt will be held in connection with the Priory Sc.hool. The following cortificat.es and prizei have b3en won during th 3 year :—OxFord L toil Seal:it-, division 1, ; Dora Price; Oxfori Lral.Linior, division 1, Dorothy Hall, Ads Isherwood. Division 2, C mstance 'Hall. Trinity Colleze, 1., vidon, Piartsforte 'playing, junior division, honours, Bessie Whitlow ; The 'Royal Draw- ing Society, division I, surface description, Honours; Lucy Skinner. Dorothy Hall, Constance Hall. Pass ; Nellie Taylor, Elizaheth Armstrong-Carter, Ida I Had(lock, Lucy Isherwood, Leonora Sankey, Agatha Jones;. Ada Isherwood, Ethel Cratnpton, Mabel Cra•upton, 1) wa, Roberts. Division 2, Fore- shortened curves and Dictated Drawing. Honours, Ada Isherwood, Constance Hall, Dorothy ' Hall, Lucy Skinner, Ida Haddock, Elizabeth Armstrong-0 trter. Pass : Mabel Crampton, Daris Williams, Ethel Crampton, Agatha .Tones, Lucy Isher- wood, Nellie Tudor. Division 111,. straight-lined perspectiv, and memory drawing. —Honours : Lucy Skinner. Dorothy Hall. Ada Isherwood, Constance Ball. Pass : .Nellio Taylor. Prizes : Class VI • Ada Isherwood ; class V. (a), Dorothy Hall • class V. (b), Ethel Crampon ; class IV., Lucy Isherwool ; class 111., Ma. Haddock; class IL. Alick Ferguson ; class I. (a), Gwen Thomas ; class I. (b), Isabel Ferguson. Examination prizes ; Ada Isherwood and Gwen Thomas. French prize : Ethel Crampton. Attendance prizes: Ada . Isherwood, Dorothy Hall, Ethel Crampton. Conduct prizes, Adi Isherwool, Dorothy Hall. Music prize - Nellie Taylor.</p>
