        <p>Oil Stoves, Coke Stoves, Lamps, &amp;u.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridse St., Warrington.—Advt. The Rev. R. Wilkins Rees. " In his introduction to "Creatures of the Night," a book in which he displays expert studies of the life histories of the otter, the water-vole, the field-vole, the fox, the hare, the badger, and the hedgehog, Mr. Alfred W. Rees says "I. am again greatly indebted to I my brother, R. Wilkins Rees. His wide and accurate, knowledge his been constantly at my disposal, and int the preparation of, these studies he has given me mach ! indispensable advice and assistance." The Rev. R. Wilkins Rees has been repeatedly urged to republish some of his literary studies as contributed to well- known journals. His recently privately priuted booklet on Edward Fitzgerald, of Omar Khayyam fame, was warmly praised by such celebrated writers as Mr. George Meredith, Mr. Rudyard Kipling, Mr. Aldis Wright, Mr. Auitin; Dobson, and Mr. A. C. Benson, and has been eulogized in the United States as well as ia this country. Mr. Wilkins Rees will be lecturing in Warrington next Mirch for the Literary and Philosophical Society, and his subject will be " Ruskin's .Style." The illustrations to "Creatures of the Night "arc by Miss Florence H. Laverock, a talented Warrington artist. TINDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS.—Advt. LADIES' BEST GLACB BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDOE-STREET.—Advt.</p>
