es Forshaw. Miss Crampton, and Mr. Frank Aukland. After dinner there were several short speeches. Mr. SUTTON (Assistant clerk to the Guardians) said he had been asked to preside at that convivial, happy Meeting on that the most important day of the Christian Year, end he had the utmost pleasure in accepting the Invitation. There were mealy Guardians who doubtless would have been present hut for family engagements, but they had been engaged in good and charitable work in other parts of the town and district. Mr. Sutton alluded to the fact that his connection with the Beard of Guardians extended over is period of nearly thirty- four years, and said he cad been in that hall on the occa- sions of the Christmas dinner as often es his health per- , witted. He could unity ill cemnliment to Mr. and Mrs. ' Awhery (the master and matron) that never before had he seen the festivities better conducted than this year. (Applause.) ' Mr. T. PfiNtiIERTON, an inmate of the House, moved a vote of thanks to the Guardians, the Master end Matron and staff, of whom be spoke in very high terms. He had been the recipient that day of a message from his mast Gracious Majesty's private secretary, with regard to his attempt to " defend the interests of the working students in public schools." lie had not met with all the success he desired by a great deal, hut, at tile SAM) time he mid received no discouragement to his efforts, which had been centinn- nits during the past two years. Ire would ", plod on until the climax came.' Mr. JOHN' PLAT r, another inmate, seconded, and the vote was enthilisattticall v accorded. Mr. T. Asitcrtorr briefly responded end wished all present a merry Christmas and a happy new year. The inmates were free to spend the afternoon pleasantly and a" special" tea was provided. The dav's festivities concluded with nn improinntu smoking concert AT THE POST OFFICE. There has been n great increase generally in the work at the Post office this Christmas—which speaks well for the trade and prosperity If Warrington. The relvenen posting system—the posting of letters in advance to ha held over for delivery on Christmas rley—was although more largely taken advantage of by the public than last year, not quite airs to expectations, but, as people become more familiarised with tha practice it will doubtless become more popular. The fieeres for the advance-posting were 17,000 as against 1,000 last Christ- mas but, it should be borne in mind that on this occa- sion the innovation einbraced the whole of the United Kingdom, whereas,in the previous year it. was restricted to local letters only, A considerable extra staff had to be employed in order to deal efficiently with the Christ- ' miss pressure and in addition, more room had to be found, by the temporary acquisition of the GyMniiaitint ereruisea and the Assembly Room rat the Conservative Club. In the former place the parcels were dealt with and in the latter the postmen found room far the pre- paration of the letters for their severe! "walks." When the new office is erected the inconvenience of moving in and out of additional premises will be obviated. Thronghont the whole of the Christmas period all mails were only dispatched and the deliveries of letters,parcel, .te... Promptly made despite the fact that there were approximately half a million of letters and 20 000 parcels to be dealt with.</p>
        <p>P AGE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>CHRISTMASTIDE.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMASTIDE.</p>
        <p>HOW IT WAS CELEBRATED LOCALLY.</p>
        <p>Christmastide, the most joyous of all seasons, was celebrated by Warringtonians with the customary whple-heartedness. Pre-eminently, a home festival, the great day is eagerly looked forward to by pang and old alike, and few indeed arthe homes which do not bear some evWence of the general rejoicing. Oae of the advantages of Christmas Day falling on Monlay was that a longer holiday than usnal was secured, alt. the works and pla3es,o.f linsiness being closed until Wednesday. Saturday was a remarkably busy day for the tradesmen. Judging from the great quantities of seasonable wares which were 'disposed of, money has been more plentiful this Christmastide than it has been for some years. During Saturday evening the markets and streets presented scenes of . anima- tion, and the shops were crowded. The ancient custom of carol-singing has not by any means fallen into desuetude, and several choirs were abroad on Katurday evening and Christmas Eve. The cold weather of Sunday give promise Of seasonable ! conditions on the morrow, bat any hopes which had • been entertained of an" old-fashioned Christmas " were (homed to disappointment for there was an almost spring-like mildness in the atmosphere on Christmas Day. Large congregations attended the , early morning services at the Anglican and Roman , Catholic churches which had been very tastefully " decorated with flowers and evergreens. At the !vurions public institutions evervting possible was done to make the day a memorable one and reports of the festivities will be found balmy. Boxing Day opened bright and sunny, and the keen, frosty air Made it pleasant to indulge in walking or cycling. !Later, however, the brightness was dispelled, and a raw, penetfating wind made the conditions somewhit cheerless. The football match at Wildersporil attracted a large gate and in the evening there were !croWded houses at the pantomime at the Royal Court Theatre and at the entertainment given in the Parr !Hall by Mr. Joshua Dyson's gipiy choir. CHRISTMAS DRUNKENNESS. !CLUEF CONSTABLE'S STATEMENT. ! There were only font cases of drunkenness for trial at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday. The Chief Constable said a popular fallacy was that people could do just as they liked at`Christmas time. To the credit of the town it must be stated that Warrington had been quieter than usual this Christmas, which showed 'that the fallacy ,he had mentioned seemed to be ! dying away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>DISTRIBUTION_ OF HOT-POT.</head>
        <p>DISTRIBUTION_ OF HOT-POT.</p>
        <p>To the deserving poor Christmas brings happiness. as I on that day they are supplied with a good meal at the expense of the !via,yor (Alderman Balton). As usual ! hot-pot and mince pies and broad wars distributed on Monday at tne Volunteer Drill Hall, from eleven to ! twelve noon, each child being also presented by Air. Henry Taylor, Police Court Aiissionary, with a Christ- mas card. The hot-pot and mince pies were prepared by Air. and Airs. Pawls, and were very tasty. Some 3001bs. of meat, 500 mince pies. Lind 700lUs. of bread were disposed of. A willing band of workers, headed by the Ai tvoress, superintend3d the wants of the appli- cants for food. SALVATION ARAW.</p>
        <p>Early on Christmas morning the band of the Salvation Army paraded the streets of the town. playing suitable selections. Hot-pet and coal were distributed among the poor of the town the tickets for which had been given by the l‘fayor net I,Lrio Greenall respe3tively. and in addition also a number of buns given by a friend were dispomri. Oa Tuesday a public tea meeting took place, about 150 sitting down to, a substantial repast. This was followed by what was riesorit.erl as a " con- gloroaration " meeting, the bawl contributing musical selections, under the leadership of Bandmaster Lee. children's drill followed, the arrangements being carried out by Adjutant Cooper. ORFORD B.A.BRACKIS.</p>
        <p>"Tommy Atkin-s-4-AIWTay-s-Jr;ntWriCCriti; grest zest into tha Christmas festivities, and at Orford Barracks the day was very happily spent. The rooms were gay with sensonsble decorations, evergreens, flags and mottoes being displayed with artistic effect. Greetings to the officers were prominent. The m ijority of the men were granted leave of alronce, thus hsing given an onner- tunity of meeting their friends or going home, Those who-stsyei in the barraoks were very well catered for the Christmas dinner consisting of turkey. geese, ham, plum puddings, fruit, ltc. In the evening A dance %VAR held. while another enjoyable function was a social promoted by the sargesnts. Praise is rine .to Colour- Sergeant Batley. Serzeant Guh, aryl ll,elunter McCue who were mainly responsible for the decorations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>INFIRNIXRY.</head>
