        <p>The war Is at the intlrmary w 're a festive garb on Christmas Day, there being a splendid display of holly and seasonable foliage, attraptiyely arranged by the no-see. As in past years a dinner of roan beef, vege- tables, and plum pudding awaited the inmates at one o'clock, followed in the evening by the special tee pro- vided by the Mines, who were untiring in their efforts to secure the comfort and enjoyment of the patients. The junior members of St. Pint's choir att.,nded as usual, and inn; a number of Christmas enrols in each ward, while in the evening vocal and inetriunental music was provided, and al t.ogother the day was spent in a very happy manner. The visitors during the day I i'ocluded, in addition to members of the medical staff. the Rev. W. and Mrs. Bracecamp. the Rey. B. Id. and Mrs. Bowen. Mrs. Lowe, Mr. and Mrs. Bailey, Mr. Bailey (Manchester), Mr. Hodskinson (Manchester), Mrs. Bracecamp, BRn , and Hr. Smith.</p>
