      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>THE...</head>
        <p>THE...</p>
        <p>WHITE ROSE.</p>
        <p>By GERALD BISS.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXXIV.</p>
        <p>THE duke stood in the old dining-hall of his famous house on the Heath, facing a blazing fire.</p>
        <p>He was dressed in the tweed suit he had put on in the morning; and in his hand he held a poker. With it he gave the logs a final stir. . .</p>
        <p>"That's all, dear," he said in a relieved voice, turning to the duchess, who was stand- ing by his side. " I have burnt every paper and possible clue to the whole of this affair. Anyhow, it will foil the police in collecting evidence against those who have fled and prevent the country from ever knowing the fullest details." His wife nodded.</p>
        <p>"It is best so, dear," she answered. "The most guilty of us in the eyes of the law must pay the penalty, but why the rest ? Have you seen. the others ? "</p>
        <p>"Yes," answered the duke a little wearily, " I have had a talk with each one of them. Mr. and Mrs. Knox catch the boat train to- nigh t ; and Islington is off on his yacht, pea- green with fear, and is taking Cordingly with Brinckley jumped at the chance of escape, and it will be a long time before he is heard of again. As for Godsmark, he has already bolted somewhere —back to Palestine, perhaps. Carruthers prefers to share our lot for reasons of his own. It is characteristic of the man and his pluck. He has played high and lost all; so, rather than own defeat, he prefers—well, you know. Sir Andrew has given me the prescription I wanted, or, rather, the medicine itself; but he decided not to take any himself, choosing in preference retirement on his well-earned savings and a most salubrious climate. Frazer, of course, took it like a man and only left me a few minutes ago to go back to his rooms and destroy some papers. •By the way, I was rather pleased by one little thing. He asked me, between man and man, feeling how little good it was to us now, for money. It's the old story of a woman in the case, and he wanted to do something for her. Luckily I had a case full of notes—ten fifties. I think; and he was most grateful. It is pleasant to feel that one is of sonic little use at this juncture, isn't it ? He will be back by eight. Mr. Stuart —his Majesty. I mean took it very coolly, so far as he himself was concerned, but was much perturbed about the rest of us. I implored him to fly while there was time; but he refused sternly and sharply. He has gone to write some letters and is returning at eight. One piece of news, by the bye. which he gave me shows that all •is up. A later edition of one of the evening papers reports that the Home Secretary himself went down to Windsor and interviewed the King in his private apartments. This sudden visit has aroused considerable speculation, and sensational developments are said to be expected. That seals our doom anyhow. But what have you been doing, dear, while I have been busy ? " . .</p>
        <p>" Writing to the children," she answered in a low voice, " asking them to forgive us, if they can. It will ruin their young lives, this awful revelation. I can see now how foolish, how wrong, how wicked, it has all been t I never dreamt what it would mean or all it would lead to."</p>
        <p>" Shush, my darling," said the duke gently, yet with a touch of his old imperiousness, "it's no good crying over spilt milk. We have been guilty of an error—an error of judgment—and we must pay for it." A knock come on the door.</p>
        <p>The duchess looked up. minutes to eight. The usual pass-word was given, and the duke unlocked it. Adlerley and Carruthers entered. Both were dressed as though for dinner, and each wore a white rose in his buttonhole.</p>
        <p>It was a few</p>
        <p>They shook hands with the. duke and duchess.</p>
        <p>" Rather early, I am afraid," said Car- ruthers in his usual lht tone ; " but we have news."</p>
        <p>" Indeed ? " said the duke interestedly. " Yes, that arch-blunderer, Branksea, at- tempted to shoot the Home Secretary in Whitehall this afternoon and then shot him- self. Perhaps it was as well," he concluded. " It is his clumsiness wide:, has ruined us all."</p>
        <p>The duchess grew wbitt to her very lips. " The beginning of • the end," she mur- mured.</p>
        <p>The next arrivals were the Earl of Struan and Lord Stepney, cool and collected, but with their faces very stern. The Marchioness of Swanage came next, haughty and erect, with her husband behind her, mottled and tottering. _</p>
        <p>" You have heard the news? " she said to the Duchess of Hampstead in a low voice. The other responded with a warm pressure of the hand. At such a time all animosities of the past were instinctively forgotten. " Poor boy," his mother went on, " this plot unhinged his mind, I believe. He has been very strange the last few days." Portcullis was next. He slouched in in his shooting suit of the morning, looking furtively round the room. " Shush," lie said, hoarsely, "there are men outside ! I saw them lurking in the shadows as I came in."</p>
        <p>The others started forward, crowding round him and questioning him, all except Carruthers.</p>
        <p>" Bah," he said, regarding the dissipated peer a trine contemptuously, "some people see things which don't exist."</p>
        <p>NBhom do we lack now? " asked the duke with a shade of anxiety in his voice. He did not know how much to believe of Portcullis's report; but he felt that the police could not be far off.</p>
        <p>The great clock had struck eight some moments before, booming through the great room.</p>
        <p>' The King, Gordon, and Frazer." answered Atherley. lookin&lt;, round. "The others are not expected. I believe." "I for one don't expect Frazer," said Car- ruthers in a cutting voice. "He is not the type of man who looks upon debts of honour as binding obligations, but payments to be postponed as long as possible or entirely to be avoided. Gordon is of the same kidney, if I'm any judge." The duke held up his hand in protest. " Mr. Carruthers, I cannot agree with you,' be said sharply. "In both these gentlemen I have entire confidence. Mr. Frazer would never have left here at all, except that he might burn some compromising papers affect- ing our other friends."</p>
        <p>The young editor bowed to the older man in deference to his opinion, but still he could not refrain from the slightest shrug of his shoul- ders.</p>
        <p>"And the King ? " asked Lord Swanage querulously. "Is he going to leave us in the lurch ? "</p>
        <p>" Sir," said the Duke of Hampstead, turn- ing even more sharply and facing him sternly, " you forget yourself. I would stake my life on the King:" " That's what we have all done," answered the other with a groan which was almost a moan, " and lost ! " His voice ended almost in a wail.</p>
        <p>Silence fell on the party as the hands of the clock crept slowly round, and a strange fear fell upon them, lest they had been fooled by the man for whom they had sacrificed every- thing. . .</p>
        <p>The clock struck singly, announcing the quarter. Muttering under his breath, Portcullis moved to the side-board and poured out some neat brandy, Lord Swanage followed his example, eager to check the quivering and chattering of his inside, which made him i .feel sick.</p>
        <p>Carruthers from force of habit lit a cigarette and inhaled a deep puff. The duke moved from one leg to the other. At last there came a knock, and the duke spying to the door. To his intense re-lief, Otto entered, debonair and ahr.cst ay in his ntmearance. He tvas</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <p>the Duke of Hampstead in ringing tones ot relief.</p>
        <p>"It will take some breaking in," he said to Montgotzierie. Just at that moment one of the men ran round from the back.</p>
        <p>Otto bowed to the company. • Ladies, your pardon," he said in his clear, pleasant voice, " gentlemen, forgive me ; I fear lam very late. You must have thought that I was turning traitor and going to play false those dear friends who have done so much for me. But I was delayed by important correspondence. That must be my excuse for such bad manners." He greeted each in turn with a cordial grip of the hand. His eyes were bright and sparkling, and never had he seemed so much at his ease.</p>
        <p>f' We've caught an old woman at the back door. who was coming .out to reconnoitre, and</p>
        <p>the door's open," he said. II Good, said the chief. t" Six of you cut round and get in there as quietly as possible while we go on ringing. If you can, get through and let us in. I don't like to leave the front in case of an attempt at escape. In little over a minute the front door was opened noiselessly by one of the men. Not a soul about or a sign of life," he reported under his breath.</p>
        <p>As, last of all, he took the duke's hand a second time and held it for an instant in his own: he whispered. _</p>
        <p>" I wonder what's up?" thought Sir Hugo, as he filed his men into the great hall. Striking a match he found the switch and turned on the light. . __</p>
        <p>"There are men outside—your police. I fancy." The duke started.</p>
        <p>"Let us to business," went on Otto, turn- ing to the company. " Are'we all here ? " "We are awaiting Mr. Frazer and Mr. Gordon, your Majesty." replied the duke, looking round uneasily. Otto laughed a trifle harshly. " You need not trouble to wait for Mr. Frazer, I fancy," he said contemptuously. " He is,! fear, a traitor and tried to tempt me, too, to betrayal. Not an hour ago he left me for the Continent via Flushing, and he was most solicitous of my company. But I declined the honour. In fact, I fear my temper* got the better of my manners and—tc cut a long story short—l kicked him out "</p>
        <p>" That door," he said, pointing to the banqueting-hall ; and at a sirm from him Montgomerie tried it, but it would not open. Then, with a truncheon, he hammered on it. "Open in the King's name." But no answer was vouchsafed to the sum- mons.</p>
        <p>" It's not so strong as it looks," he reported to Sir Hugo " it's old and a bit worm-eaten. We can break it in pretty easily." " All right," answered Verschovle. " I'll stand by the switch and as it gives I'll turn off the light, or they'll have a clear sight of us, and they won't hesitate to shoot. Go for the switch in the room yourself and put the light on suddenly for one second to see how they are disposed." With a nod Montgomerie marshalled half a dozen of the heaviest of his men and gave them the word.</p>
        <p>A murmur ran round the room, and Car- ruthers smiled cynically. " We will not wait for Mr. Gordon," said the duke abruptly: and without another word the small party took their seats round the table, waiting only till Otto had placed him- self at the head and motioned to them to sit down.</p>
        <p>The first time they rushed at it, the door creaked and groaned, but did not give; nor the second. The third time, however, there was a cracking and rending ; and as Sir Hugo switched off the light it went both at the hinges and the lock, and the men were pre- cipitated into the room, one on top of the other, all rolling on the floor.</p>
        <p>At a sign from Otto the Duke of Hamp- stead rose to speak, his face grimly set. " Your Majesty," he said, speaking in a metallic voice, "it is not pleasant to report failure, so do not let us dwell upon it. More- over, the time is short, it seems. The house appears to be surrounded, and at any moment we may find ourselves prisoners, to be held up to public ridicule and misunderstanding, fore- doomed to a disgraceful death. There is only one alternative; so let us take it. On that tray," he went on, pointing to a silver salver holding a decanter and some wine glasses. " stands the solution of the problem. Sir Andrew Wentworth, whom I do not blame, as I do not blame most of those who have pre- ferred exile—indeed, it was in most cases vont Majesty's urgent with and mine—Sir Andrew Wentworth this afternoon gave me a bottle which he has by arrangement had ready for some time in view of an emergency. It is a new poison almost unknown. Its action is swift, painless, and—the only alternative I I will not say more, your Majesty. I will not even express regret for failure. We must accept the inevitable. As you know, sire, we have been loyal and are loyal to the end. but the stars in their, courses have fOught against us. But to die in the service of your Majesty whom we have all learnt to love for himself, for his graciousness, for his charm of Manner, is of itself honour and reward indeed."</p>
        <p>Under the door they felt a body. To Verschoyle's surprise there was no fusillade or ping of revolver bullets, as he had expected : nor was there a single sound. In an instant Montgomerie was inside the door, groping up and down the wall by the flickering light of the fire till his hand touched the switch.</p>
        <p>Turning to face the room, he put the light full on for a second, as directed by -Verschoyle, and promptly switched it off again ; but with that single gleam of light he had seen a strange sight—one which he did not fully compre- hend.</p>
        <p>The room seemed ghostly with nothing but the flickering flame of light half revealing, half distorting the scene, as it alternately flared up and died down again. He paused an instant and then again switched the light on full. Round the table were seated men and women—some had fallen forward, one or two had slipped sideways off their chairs. One was seated in a great chair at the head of the table in an easy natural attitude, a white rose in his buttonhole, and one lay prone on the floor.</p>
        <p>Towards the end of his speech the duke had worked himself up and at the close sat down glowing and enthusiastic once 'mire for the lost cause; and, as lie tt down, he took his wife's hand in his.</p>
        <p>• With a bound Sir Huge was at his side, and they stood together, drinking in the strange sight as the men picked themselves up off the shattered door.</p>
        <p>Verschoyle and Montgommie approached the table and its ghostly company, and the latter laid his hand on "Mr. Stuart."</p>
        <p>" Morituri te salutint." murmured Car- ruthers, cool and cynical to the end, whilst the others watched Atherley with fascinated eyes, as with a steady hand and without a tremor he' tilled the little glasses one by one. " One moment, dear friends," said Otto, rising, " one moment in which to thank you, one and all. My heart is too full for words. We have all fought in the same cause—and lost: and I thank you from the bottom of my heart. The Stuarts ever were a foredoomed race, and tragedy has overhung their whole history. But somehow some have loved them loyally, nonetheless, and they were ever grateful for it. We are treading the path which others have trod before us in the same unlucky cause. I can say no more. Dear friends, I thank you."..</p>
        <p>" He's warm, sir ; he's quite warm," he exclaimed excitedly. " But they're all dead," said Sir Hugo grimly," nevertheless. Can't you see what it is, man It's suicide, wholesale suicide ! And perhaps it's better so." he concluded under his breath.</p>
        <p>"There's another right under the door, sir," said one of the awe-stricken men, coming forward; "and we've rolled on top of him. I guess he was the one who turned off the light." And they turned over the mangled corpse of the imperturbable Atherley, crushed almost out of recognition.</p>
        <p>LT° be concluded.)</p>
        <p>And, as he resumed his seat, Otto murmured the single-souled motto of his life and death, "For Rudy's sake." At that moment cam,!, the clang of the great outside bell, furiously pealed. A second later it was repeated.</p>
