      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>CHAPTER XXXV. HARD on the heels of Otto's car followed</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXXV. HARD on the heels of Otto's car followed</p>
        <p>another motor.</p>
        <p>hilt sat Verschoylc, grim end determined. Ahead of him he scented battle and felt that it might not he bloodl3ss.• , The desperate attempt on the Home Soeretary showed him that the conspirators would stick at nothing; and he knew that they were at the end of their tether.</p>
        <p>Opposite the house the car pulled up sharply, and he sprang off with two inspect- ors, ordering the man to drive on out of sight.</p>
        <p>_ The little party made swiftly for the shadow of the high wall on the far side of the gate: There they found others waiting.</p>
        <p>"Is Montgonierie there ? " asked the chief in a low tone. 'Here, sir, mand. " And</p>
        <p>Verschoyle drew him aside. "Is all ready " he whispered. how many of them have arrived ? " " Eight," replied Montgotnerie, • answering the second question first. " one of them a woman. The Duke and Duchess of Hamp- stead Are inside, Williams reports : and there may be one or two more, but I fancy not. People have been coming and going ever since the men got here at live o'clock." " So far so good," -said Vechoyle. the preparations ? " " I have twenty-four men up here besides ourselves. Some are posted round the house, but report no sign of life, not even a glint of light, so we can't locate them." "The banqueting - hall, I fancy," said Sir Hugo, " the door on the right as you enter the big hall. I know the house, and that's certainly the most likely room. Anyhow, we'll try it first:" " There's a ladder over the wall," went on Montgomerie, " but we don't need it. as we've caught the old porter and gagged him. I let the last one in myself, but didn't recognise him. I don't imagine any more are g as all except this one have been here since about eight o'clock." " I don't want any more, either," said Ver- schoyle quickly ; "we only want the ring- leaders. So let's get to work." In a minute or two they were all inside the garden, and Sir Hugo silently collected his men at the side of the house. Then he ad- dressed them in a low voice.</p>
        <p>"And</p>
        <p>"We shall probably have to break our way in. my men," he said. "and we may have a tough fight for it. We arc dealing with the ringleaders of the 'White Rose' gang"—at the sound of the name even the most seasoned of them gave a perceptible start and ,all rose to the importance of the occasion with a feeling of exhilaration—" so vou can guess how desperate they are. Stick at nothing, as your own lives depend on it, and when you hit, hit hard. We'll try the banqueting-hall first— the first room on the right." Having posted sentries, the chief led his men round to the front door. Near it stood several motors, grim and ghostly in the frosty moonlight. Sir Hugo took a last look round to make sure that his men were. ready, and saw their faces pale and expectant in the clear white light.. Then he pulled the great bell with all his might. "Open in the King's name," he cried. Waiting a second, he repeated the ring and the summons.</p>
        <p>But there was no answer, save the re- sounding echo of the old bell through tite dark house. _</p>
        <p>lie shook the great oak door, but realised that that was no good.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>PERILS OF OUTDOOR WORK.</head>
        <p>PERILS OF OUTDOOR WORK.</p>
        <p>Rheumatism and Piles Afflict a</p>
        <p>N.E.R. Guard.—Zam-Buk Cures.</p>
        <p>• Those who have to brave all weathers find Zara-link an ideal remedy for the season's perils. Al r.J.B.Thorup- SOT, of 19, Mill-street, Ilartlepool. a North Eastern railway guard, said to a " South Dunham Mercury " reporter "After a long stretch of work in cold and wet weather, I suffered severely with unbearable rhea- I rustic pains in the back, and piles. I was obliged to , leave off work. I could scarcely eat or sleep on account of these pains. I NW two medical men, but, got no better. Eventsally I heard about Zant-ltuk, and sent fora box. I applied the balm as directed,and then got the first relief I had found during several months of snffering. The aching pains and the piles. gradually disappeared, and by the time I had used a second box I aas able to resunie my employment. I am completely cured of the rheirinnt ism arid piles, and my general bodily health is better. I gladly seize the opportunity of praising Zsm-Ruk." liubbing Zam-liuk in rubs pain, stiffness, or tV en kness out. It should always be used for soreness in joints or , nmscle, sprains, strains, back pains, rheumatism, cramp, swellings, piles, inflathrhaticm,bait knees and stikles,crits, burns, scalds, poisoned or festering sores, eczema, psorissis, ulcers, bad legs, deceased ankles, sore backs, itch, dandruff, scurf, scalp cores, erysipelas, piles, rashes, abscesses, boils, pimples, ringworm, etc. Of all chemists or the Zarn-Buli Co., 4, Red Cross Street, London, E.C., for Is. Id. per box, or 2s, 9d. for large faintly size , (2s. Od. bot contains nearly 4 times the ls.; 11d).</p>
        <p>• PADGIATE COTrAGE HOMES. To the children in . the Padgate Cottage homes Christmas is the happiest of days and Monday was no exception to the rule. The schoolroom and the interior of the whole of the buildings presented a pleasing appearance being tastefully decorated with holly and evergreens, with garlands of paper flowers. A dinner consisting of roast beef, potatoes, vegetables, and plum padding was served to the scholars-96 boys and 111 girls—the meal being much enjoyed. Oranges, apples, swsets and nuts were afterwards distributed to every child. A good number of the old boys a...d girls visited the Homes during some portion of the day. Among • those present at the dinner were Mr. O. Mather ( chair- man of the Cottage Unites Committee), Sir. William Houghton, guardian, and ilev. Pollock-Hill, chaplain. Cheers were given for the Guardians and office's. In the evening an entertainment was held, at which the Superintendent (Mr. A. Wright) gave a stump speech, taking as his ideal the Scotch man who never was beaten an:: a recitation from one of Sir Walter Scott's poems. Several children sang very sweetly different school songs und others. The entertainment concluded with it rollicking farce arranged by the otticers,aud the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>No cocoa is pure which contains substances not naturally present in the Cocoa Bean. Many of the so-called cocoas now on sale are merely cocoa preparations, containing additions of such things as skimmed milk, starch. sugar, hops, etc., which lessen the quality of cocoa, and therefore give an inferior beverage. To make sure of obtaining pure cocoa, ask for and see that you get "Cadburys." Cocoa is stroncest when pure, and in its natural state contains all the elements necessary for physical well being. Cadbury's cocoa is abso- lutely pure, and contains no added ingredients whatever.</p>
        <p>There is no cocoa so easy of digestion and assimilation as Cadbury's cocoa.</p>
