      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>," answered his second-in-com-</head>
        <p>," answered his second-in-com-</p>
        <p>BY DR. MACNAMARA, M.P.</p>
        <p>with the appalling misrepresentations under the cloak of which it has been sought to make the Chinese Ordinance palatable to the British public the fact that the Colonial Office had to issue at the beginning of th!s year a warning that workmen were not to go to the Transvaal, where there was a considerable amount of distress in</p>
        <p>the towns and country districts, "owing to the general. want of employment." The warning concluded: "There is no demand for white miners, of whom there are large numbers on the spot without work !"</p>
        <p>But: the climax of audacity in regard to this matter is the apology which the Tate Govern- ment put forward in extenuation of its policy. Substantially both Mr. Balfour and Mr. Lyttelton have stated that they would not have put their hand to this Ordinance had it not been that the coolie, was expressly desired by the people of the Transvaal. Now what are the facts? TI e Transvaal is governed by a Legislative Council of taenty-seven persons, not one of whom is an eleetsd representative. Thirteen ate pH Go‘evnment officials. and fourteen are non.rffieal Goveinment nominees. The Chineke Orclinni C wns carried at the Legislative Council by eighteen votes to four. The four against were non-official Government nominees, and tht,--e'ghteon pemons by whom the Ordinance was erlorted comprised thirteen paid Government ,officials and five non-official Government nominees. And this is, if you please, "the express wish of the people of the Transvaal!"</p>
        <p>All this is bad enough, but national shame and abasement are not complete until we contemplate the treatment which the coolie has received. The 1,700 desertions, and the 21,000 cases of "unlawful absence from work," are eloquent testimony as to the nature of the treatment which is their daily portion under the British flag. That this should have been the sequel to a war which involved so much expenditure of life and treasure represent the gravest blot on the record of the present administration. At the instance, as I say, of unscrupulous greed, they have written the words "Chinese Slavery" across the name of "Wilberforce',' in the annals of the race. They have impudefitly violated the spirit which has made the British people great. They have cynically trampled noon the British tradition in favour of Justice and Freedom. The soldier in South Africa buried the Union Jack after Majuba. It would have been better to have left it where it was than to have dug it up to put this stain upon it. In reward for his patience, endurance, chivalry, and gallantry, the soldier sees the shadow of the Chinese com- pound fall across the grave of his dead com- rade. And if the Liberal Party comes into office charged by public opinion with no other duty, it is charged by the outraged conscience of the people of this country to bring this ugly thing to an end as rapidly as possible.</p>
      </div>
