      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>My Dear Children,—</head>
        <p>My Dear Children,—</p>
        <p>Now that you are in the midst of your holidays, I hope that you will all thoroughly enjoy yourselves. I expect that many of you are hoping for skating and snowballing during January before you start your lessons again.</p>
        <p>I must thank W. E. Turney for a Very pretty little painting, and I have sent him a picture post card album for sending me in a list of twenty-four new 'names for the League. I hope many more of you will try and win one of these pretty albums, as I ant very anxious to have a thousand names in about •a month. We now have just six hundred members. I am very pleased to welcome Miss E. Barnett as a new member, and thank her for her letter. I was also very pleased with the letter and contribu- tion from Connie Saunders. It is too long to print this week, but I shall try and insert half of the story next week, and finish it the following. I hope you will all write me a .good ac- count of how you spend your holidays, and what You all did on Christmas Day. With all good wishes for a very bright and happy new, year, • - •</p>
        <p>From UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>TH CHRISTMAS GIFT.</p>
        <p>Great excitement prevailed in the school- room. Jack and Marie wondered if the • morning would ever end. How slowly the hands of the clock went round, and the French verbs had never seemed quite so hard. There was certainly sufficient cause for excitement, for that morning, Irfore going away, Uncle Fred had given them a bright new half-crown each for a Christmas present, and their mother had said they might spend it just how they liked. . • At last the clock struck twelve, and, put- ting down • their books, almost before their governess had finished speaking and given them permission to go, they rushed out, and ran downstairs to the drawing-room. t Their .mother looked up quite startled as the door burst' open. "Why, chick', what is the matter ? " she) said. "Oh, mother! " began Marie, quite OUtp of breath. "Please may, we go to town. this afternoon to spend. our money?" "Yes, mother," chimed in Jack, "and may we go by ourselves?" Their mother smiled. "Well, if you will promise to be quite good, and come home early, you may go," she said; "but now run off, for nurse will be waiting dinner for you." After many thanks, and a parting hug, the children ran off, and at dinner they talked of nothing but what they should buy. As soon as the meal was over, and they, were warmly wrapped in their' coats, they set off. The town was very bright, and the Shop windows were brilliant with the Christmas goods of all descriptions. Oh, how difficult it was to know what to buy! When they had chosen one thing, there was always. something they liked still better. As they were standing in front of a large cafe,. Marie suddenly turned around, and saw a sight that made her start. Standing near the window, and looking with longing eyes at the tempting rows of cakes', was a girl of about her own -age. She had no hat nor stockings on, and her poor little blue toes could be seen through the broken shoes, and the white little face was pinched and wan. Marie impulsively ran into the shop, and. putting down her money, asked for sixpenny- worth of buns. With these she hurried out and ran after the girl, who looked up quickly as Marie pushed the bag into her arms. Who can describe the light of joy that sprang. into the child's eyes? Marie, see- ing it, could never • forget. Before the girl could say a word, Marie had run back to where jack was standing. The shout had no longer any attraction, and the children thoughtfully turned home. Neither said much, but ever before their eyes rose the white face of the child. When they arrived home, their mother had gone out, and the children sat quietly for some time in front of the schoolroom re, until the nurse came in with a paper in her hand, which she gave them. It was the Christmas number of a maga- zine. and in it they saw an appeal for money to give some of the London waifs a good Christmas dinner. As soon as their mother returned, she was met by the two eager children, and, when she had hoard the whole storv, gladly gave her consent that they might send their money.</p>
        <p>Did they regret it? Well, when they thought of the things they were to have bought, the picture of hundreds of poor, starving little children enjoying a Christmas dinner made them quite happy again, and on Christmas morning their mother had prepared a little surprise, for waiting for them were two brown paper parcels, with a label attached to each :—" From Mother and Father. 'lt is more blessed to give than to receive.' "</p>
        <p>ELSIE HUGHES. A CHRISTMAS STORY.</p>
        <p>Christmas is a very happy time. Children hang up their stockings, when they go to bed at Christmas Eve, for Santa ,Claus to fill with toys, books, and sweets. One Christmas Eve some little Brownies wished to make a jolly Christmas in the village; so one, • called Peterkin, cried, "Come, Brownies! Away to Work. This .is Christ- mas. Eve, you know. We'll do our best— just our level best—to give the folks a jolly Christmas." "We will so," they shouted. "Hooray 4 " and tossed their caps to the roof of the mushroom, beneath which they were standing. and then Peterkin told them what they had to do. Trippetty must go to Dame Dorothy's cottage,. and up the chim- ney, and see what makes it smoke -so atro- ciously. If it's a brick in the way, move 'it so that the old woman can boil her Christ- mas pudding. And Harumscarum can creep into the squire's pocket, just as he's wish- ing Widow Brown a merry Christmas, and tuck half-a-sovereign into his hand instead of a sixpence. And . Squaretoes can .carry the children's crumbs to the hole in the wall, so that Bob and. Jennie need not come outdoors this bitter weather. ' And Merry- heart must see that the very best in Santa's bag goes into Johnnie's stocking. And Winkle can go into grandfather's clock, and stop the hands, so that the children can keep on dancing. "And what will you do. captain?" "Oh ! never fear. As long ;,s there's a sad heart in our village, Peterkin won't be idle."</p>
        <p>LILIAN COATES. -" SANTA CLAUS. Whether it freezes, whether it thaws, Over the roofs comes Santa Claus, Driving his team Swift as a dream, ' Jingle of bells .in a cloud of steam. • He has a • sleigh that's like the wind, • In spite of the parcels piled behind— Goodies and toys, ' Meant for the joys Only of good little . girls and boys. So the Saint, Queer and quaint, Comes without ringing or ,knocking, And he strews, Down the flues, Gifts that you find in your stocking. L. C.</p>
