      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>SOME FAMOUS CHIUSTMAS PIES.</head>
        <p>SOME FAMOUS CHIUSTMAS PIES.</p>
        <p>I suppose all of you boys and girls have read the old ditty-, telling how once four- and-twenty blackbirds were baked in a pie, and how, when the pie was opened, the birds began to sing—and .they have wisely considered the story a very impossible one, but it is not without some foundation, aftPr all. A common dish on Queen Elizabeth's table, at Christmas and other great festivi- ties, was, we are told, a monster pie, from Which, when onened, there flew a numb2r of birds that, lighting in various parts of the dining-room, used to sing sweetly tothe guests at table. . .</p>
        <p>Another famous pie made its appearance at an entertainment given by the Duke of 13.uckingham to Queen Henrietta, the wife of Charles the First of Eneland. When the crust was removed, one Geoffrey Hudson, a tiny dwarf dressed to represent Santa Claus, stood revealed to the astonished com- pany. Still another celebrated Christmas pie was made in 176. for Sir Henry 'Grey. It was "composed of two bushels of flour, twenty pounds of butter, four geese, two turkeys, two rabbits, two wild clucks, two woodcocks, six snipes, for partridges, two ncats' tongues, two curlews, seven blackbird,, and seven</p>
        <p>_ This culinary marvel, as one may well call it, was about nine feet in circurrfcr,,nce: it, weighed two hundred pounds., and several .strong men were required to bear it safely to the table.• _</p>
        <p>E. M. C.</p>
      </div>
