        <head>TnE (UNREAL ELECTION.</head>
        <p>TnE (UNREAL ELECTION.</p>
        <p>The General Election naturally monopolises attention just now, and even in our Church life such is the importance of the issues at stake that sve cannot forget the coming conflict. Apart from the ordinary political issues of Pro- tection and Free Trade, &amp;c., there is one dominant issue in which the Free Churches are keenly interested. The Education Acts of 1902-3 were passed in spite of the strongest Noncon- formist Opposition, and it was then announced that the Free Churches would oppose legislation which cuts at the heart of their liberties. Talking alone will never amend the Education Acts, and Free Churchmen now feel it their duty to use their votes at the poll in order to remedy what they believe to be grave injustice. Accordingly, the National Free Church Council is taking strong action to bring the Education Question to the front at the forthcoming Elections, and a plan of an Electoral Campaign has been devised for this purpose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>CAMPAIGN OF LITERATURE.</head>
        <p>CAMPAIGN OF LITERATURE.</p>
        <p>The National Free Church Council has pre- pared literature on all phases of the Educa- tion Question, bringing out clearly and strongly the Free Church case in favour of the amend- ment of the law. It is probable that millions of leaflets will be distributed during the coining contests. In addition, posters have been pre- pared, illustrated by "F.C.G.," bringing out strongly the injustice of the present situation. Local Free Church Councils, of which there are some 900 in England and Wales, will have the management of the Campaign in the different constituencies. Candidates are being asked to pledge themselves publicly to support the Free Church position on Education.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>GREAT DEMONSTRATIONS.</head>
        <p>GREAT DEMONSTRATIONS.</p>
        <p>Great demonstrations have been arranged at Bristol, Birmingham, Newcastle-on-Tyne, and other centres with foremost Free Churchmen as speakers, whilst smaller meetings will be held in every part of the country. In other ways the Free Churches will be showing their deter- mination to put an end to the mischievous features of the Education law now that the opportunity is at hand.</p>
        <p>MOTOR-CAR C AMPAION.</p>
        <p>One of the most interesting features of the Free Church Council's propaganda will be a, series of motor-car tours through England by Free Church leaders. Immediately the election is declared motor-ears will start simultaneously from London and Newcastle-on-Tyne to tour through the constituencies, hearing fe-,ch sneakers as Dr. Clifford. Rev. F. B. Meyer, Rev. C. Silvester Horne, Rev. A. T. Gutterv. and Rev. 'Thomas Yates. The speakers will join the cars in relays. and meetinffs will be orga- nised on village-greens and market-places in the day time and in public halls in the even- ings. Cars have been engaged identical with those used by General Booth on hi 3 memorable tour.</p>
        <p>THE FORTHCOMING ANNUAL COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>In spite of the political situation, ordinary work has to be done as usual, and if it can he called ordinary work to arrange the programme for such a great series of meetings as will be behl in Birmingham next March, ibis certainly work that has been done well. The programme of the Eleventh National Free Church Council is not fully complete, hut a few details are obtainable. The meetings will be held on March 5. 7, and 8. tinder the presidency of Rev. J. Scott Lidgett, M.A. The preachers at these gatherings are always some of the most notable pulpit orators in Nonconformity This sear they will include : ROI% C. F. Aked, D.D.. Tt.sv. R, F. "[eon, NT.A., D.D., flew. G. A Johnston Ross, NI.A.. R`l% John Watson, D.D. (lan Maclaren) In addition to those sermons at the Council. special Preaching Services will also be held in Birmingham. when sermons will be delivered by Rev. J. Flanagan, Rev. C. IT. Kelly (President. Wesleyan Methodist Conferenee). Rev. J. B. Meharry, D D.. Rev. Thomas Phillips. 8.A.. Rev. A mbroe Shepherd, D.D., Rev. John Thomas, M.A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>INTERESTING SUBJECTS.</head>
        <p>INTERESTING SUBJECTS.</p>
        <p>The National Council will have for its con. sideration many important and interesting suhiee.ts. Of course, the Ellin:Minn Question will loom largely other questions include a National„ Temperance Policy, the SAWtatli Question. Morality in Public Life (introduced by Mr. T. W. Russell. M.P.), a National Simoltaneons Open Air 'Mission, &amp;e. On the onestion of Open Air Preaching the Rev. George Hansen, P.D., (1(AI.; with its Necessity, and the Rev. C. Ensnr Welters with " Methods ami Training." "The Pines, of Soiritual E•cperienca in the Msking of Thenlogy," hy the Rey. P. T. Forsyth, M.A., D.D,, is another paper which should he heard with keen interest, The De- votional Session. which is always held at the close of the meetings, will he addressed by Rev. G.•Camphell Morcan. D.D., Dr. Robertson Nicoll, and Rev. J. S. Simon.</p>
        <p>THE FREE CITUECIIVIS AND THE UNEMPLOYED.</p>
        <p>The Unemployed Question is regarded most seriously by the Free Churches, and an effort will he made at the Birmingham Meetings to add some practical contrihotion to the settle- ment of the question. Professor A. E. Garvie, D.D.. is to speak on "What the Churches can do to Solve the Problem!' and it is probable that Mr. Will Crooks. M.P., will introduce the subject: " What the State can do to Salve the Problerri."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>FREE CHURCH PRINCIPLES.</head>
        <p>FREE CHURCH PRINCIPLES.</p>
        <p>Considerable imnetus has been given of late to the study of Free Church Principles, and many local Free Church Councils are arranging classes for this purpose. For instance, the Brecon Free Church Connell is arranging an examination in Free Church History for its Young People. They have decided to use Rev. C. Sitvester Horne's book. " Nonconformity iii the Nineteenth Century,' as the text-hook. This hook is published by the National Free Church Council at Is. Q., and is eminently suited for the purpose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>AN EXTENSIVE PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>AN EXTENSIVE PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>Rev. J. Scott lidgett will soon he taking up his work as President of the National Free Church Connell. He is determined that the Free Cburchos shall not suffer during his year of office by his new responsibilities as Alderman of the London County Council. He will pay at; least one visit to each of the fifty Free Church Federations throughout the country. awl he expects on each of these visits to take part in some three or four mei-tinge. Mondays, Thurs- days. and Fridays he purposes to devote chiefly to Free Church Council work, and Tuesdays and Wednesdays to the L.C.C. Mr. Lid,c,..ett is accustomed to heavy burdens. Besides being warden of the Bermondsey Settlement, he has a Methodist minister's cares, and every week he preaches twice to the same congregation. In his moments of leisure—few though they must he—he is engaged in writing a new theological treatise.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>DR. HORTON'S CHALLENGE.</head>
        <p>DR. HORTON'S CHALLENGE.</p>
        <p>Tn his latest volume, "The Reunion of Eng- lish Christendom," Dr. Horton. in dealing with the debt the Established Church owes to Nonconformity, says he ventures to make a challenge—" Examine in any parish throughout the country the clergy, the teachers, the visitors, the workers who are doing anything for the real progress of religion, and, if your exnerience is like mine, you will find that about half of them are Dissenters by origin." Dr. Horton cites striking examples in support of his case.</p>
