      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>LETT- ERS TO THE EDITOR</head>
        <p>LETT- ERS TO THE EDITOR</p>
        <p>- - CORRESPONDENTS are particularly requested to send letters intended for Fridsy's " Examiner " not later than Thursday morning.</p>
        <p>Another Chamberlain Myth. Sir.—One of the Chamberlain myths which seem hard to kill is the statement that the increase of our trade with the Colonies disguised the fact that we are losing our trade with foreign countries. This myth was again brought forward by Earl Percy in the Parr Hallmseting. The actual figures of the three last years speak for themselves : U.K. Exports to Foreign Countries and British , Possessions (in -2 million.) , British Produce. British Manufactures. Foreign British Foreign British Countries. Possessions Countries Possessions 19Q2. 174.3 109.1 132.5 93.0 1903. 179.7 111.1 1311.9 97.8 1901. 188.8 111.9 145.1 94 4 Increase 14.5 2.8 12.9 3.4 I have not at hand the figures of the last twenty years, the period Earl Percy referred to, but it is singular that he should make the statement knowing as he ought to have done that the most up-to-date figures 'flatly contradict the contention, and this contradiction is most emphatic in the last year. Probably Earl Percy now that he is relieved from ministerial responsibility will have time to bring his arguments up to date. CHARLES E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>Penketh, December 27th, 1905.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>Sugar Tax.</head>
        <p>The Sugar Tax.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Parker's letter is in no sense a reply to what I said—that the Liberals never mention the Sugar Tax now. A few months ago they were always dragging this in, and they had not the frankness of Mr. Parker to admit that the increased price of sugar was due to a shortage of crop—and to speculation. They always attributed the advance to the tas and the abolition of the bounties. Mr. Parker admits a shortage of the crop by 20 per cent. Well, he and every other bssiness man knows that when there is a shortage of anything the speculators step in and endeavour to corner it for of course, their own benefit. Sometimes they succeed, and sometimes it ends in their undoing. We had a notable instance of this a year or two since in the case of cotton. There was a short supply and it was cornered by speculators with the result that most of tho cotton mills of the world went on short time. However, a year after there was a bountiful crop of cotton and the nails went on full time and the cotton districts of Lancashire became happy and prosperous. And so with sugar. We have had a good crop and sugar is cheap again—wonderfully cheap—grocers are selling beautiful white, English-refired sugar at 11,d. per lb, and yet the duty is still there and the bounties are abolished. I believe that in the near future we shall have sugar still cheapen—Yours, etc.</p>
        <p>111</p>
        <p>II</p>
        <p>E</p>
        <p>JOHN G'.I.NDY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="4" unit="words">
