        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>Mr. F. Monks and Liberalism.</head>
        <p>Mr. F. Monks and Liberalism.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Thanks for publishing my letter of the 13th inst., and the replies of both candidates. Mr. Pierpoint's reply is clear and intelligible, and the man in the street will have no difficulty in under- standing it. Mr. Crosfleld's reply reduced to plain English I take to mean :-- Ist. That be is a Home Ruler, and quite prepared to vote with Mr. Campbell-Bannerman, but so lung as Conservatives add Unionists will vote Liberal he is prepared to give and take a bit to secure their support. 2nd. That he is in favour of continuing the present fiscal policy, and would not favour a Conference to consider any amendment, especially that suggested by Mr. Chamberlain. 3rd. For the present he does not consider a Redistribu- tion 13111 necessary. The display of political bitterness and hatred of Mr. Chamberlain is quite unnecesaaryand the personal refer- ence to my Liberalism is easily understood. As there seems to be some doubt in his own mind, I can assure him that I arn as good a Liberal as ever and a thorough Pree Trade: ; but I understand free trade to mean free to exports as well as to imports, and I am old enough to remember that this was intended by the advocates of free trade. In those days tariffs were for revenue only, and both Cobden and Bright and the Free Trade League honestly balleved that tariffs would be abolished sidle world came to see the advantages of a free exchange of commodities. Instead of the foreigner adopting free trade in this sense, he has built up a wall of protection against us, and never hesitates to tell us that he does it to exclude our products in favour of his own. He has not been slow to perceive that industrial enterprise not only finda more employruent,latit pays better,than agriculture. The workingman finds that he can earn better wages and is content to pay the extra cost of living, and when he gets short of work in his own country, he proceeds to dump his surplus production in this country, making bad times worse thee they otherwise would be for us. The effect on our own labour market is beginning to be felt, as the labourer leaves the land and (tomes to swell the unakilled labour of our large towns, making it that the supply is greater than the demand, and reducing the wages of both, and what is perhaps worse, driving the older and weaker man out of the market in favour of the younger aid stronger men. The refusal of the Liberal party to consider the fiscal question on its own merits, and their claim that the prosperity of the past 50 years is due to free imports,is apolitical blunder. To ignore the economic advantages resulting from the development of our railways, our mercantile marine, our improved banking system, and other causes, is not a sign of that political wisdom which can see at round a question affecting national interests. The burden of local taxation for Poor Laws and Education, which are more national than local charges, will not be borne indefinitely,nor will the income tax payer be content to , continue a war tax for the benefit of the foreigner. It is much easier to complain of increased taxation than it is to reduce it, and I doubt whether any sane man' expects the new Government to do any better than the late one, in its financial administration. It is not Protection that is wanted so much as the power for our own Government to make the best bar- gain they can with those who exclude our products and to make such arrangements as will prevent successful dumping in times of depressios. If we can so arrange matters with our own Colonies as to give us some preferential treatment, such as the Canadians have done, I believe we shall find plenty of employmeni at good wages, without really increasing the cost of living.—Yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>F. MONKS.</p>
        <p>Walton Old Hall, Dec. 2Sth, 1905. -- Liberal Inconsistencies. Sir,—l was very pleased to rend Dr. Markers letter in your last issue. lam very glad that he joins me in wishing this T.,estion to be debated in a gentlemanly and courteous spirit. Like him I have been deeply pained to hear, and read the coarse personalities in- dulged in by those who happen to differ from Mr. Chamberlain, mid I hope this correspondence may have some little effect in checking in Warrington, at least, such utterances as those of Mr. Alderman Pierpoint. And now, to come to the gist of Dr. Markel's letter touching Free Trade v. Protection as practised by the firm of which he is so able and esteemed a member. Really be is refreshingly candid. Ho admits that "competition is not always for the public good." He says the principle has come to be accepted that employers may not com- pete with one another and force down prices without considering the demands for a living wage." He further says that competition leads to reducing the quality of the goods manufactured. He says that the Association of which his firm is a member is one for mutual protection and the result that they are under no necessity either to reduce the wages of their work. people or to rednee the quality of their goods but on the contrary it enables thorn to produce a better article. Now, I don't think Dr. Markel could give a better illustration of the benefits of Protection. I really could not wish for abetter one and if this is good for, the individual firm, for their workpeople and the traders who sell their goods—of whom I am one—why should it not be equally good for the nation at large? To return for a moment to this question of reducing the quality of goods, it is an undoubted fact that competition does this. Speak- ing as a tradesman of many years' standing, I may say that this is one of the banes of a tradesman's life. It is competition, competition at every hand; fierce and relentless ; and it assuredly does not lend itself to supplying the finest quality or the best articles. Dr. Markel says that the only remedy is education, physical, mental, and moral. Let me say here that War- rington owes a good deal to him in this respect. I admire—nnd so does every intelligent person in the town —his untiring efforts in this direction. But in urging this does the doctor not proceed on a false assumption ? [sit lack of brains that we suffer from? I hope I am not misunderstanding him—but I have heard this argument trotted out so very often. As a matter of fact Germans Frenchmen and Americans have brains as well is we have. Indeed the statement has been made again and again that it is in this respect we are so very much behind other countries. I have been told of the mar- vellous equipment of electrical and eneineering works in America and the continent and the cry is" Wake up. John Boll." Well, suppose, John Bull does wake up and gets on an equality with these competitors of his in this respec surely he is entitled to compete with them on equal terms. With all their superior education and business and industrial alertness do they rely on these as their sole or main weapons? Not they indeed! They protect their own trade and their own Industries. John Bull must do the same if he intends to keep what trade he has now cot. But what a curious anomaly this so- called Free Trade is. We really have'nt any Free Trade. We have'nt really oven free imports. Strangely enough we tax thins we don't produce and let in free other things which enter directly into competition with our own productions. We tax sugar to the tune of 23 per cent.; the cheaper kinds of tea—those consumed by the very poorest people—over 100 per cent. We tax chicory, cocoa, coffee, currants, figs, plums, tobacco,and a number of other things—things we don't produce here</p>
        <p>but yet which practically everybody in the land uses, the very poorest as much as the richest. To me it is nothing less than nonsense, this cry about taxing the food of the people. As a purveyor of foods I know what I am talking about when I say that these articles II have enumerated are practically as much the food of , the people as anything else that I sell, with the one exception of tobacco, and even this has become so much of a necessity that I have heard any number of working- men declare that they would rather go without their breakfhst than without their pipe of tobacco. I feel, Mr. Editor, that I have taken up a good deal of your space already, but I shall be glad if you will kindly allow me to send you one more letter for your next issue. I thank Dr. Markel for the marked courtesy of his reply to me.—Yours, etc.</p>
        <p>JOHN GANDY.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Crosfield and Foreign Labour. Sir,—l flinch regret that the Conservative party have issued a placard which appeals to the electors of War- rington to vote against Mr. A. EL Crostield because he is" one who employs a large number of foreigners." I thought that by this time the facts regarding the nationality of the people employed by Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Limited, were so well known that the " foreigner " bogey had long ago been laid to rest. In order however to remove any lingering suspicion that the unfair and untrue statement above quoted may have caused, I feel it my duty in the interests of fair play both to Mr. A. H. Crosfield and to the firm of which I am one of the 'lanaging Directors to place the simple facts before the electors of this Borough. Our firm at the present time atiords permanent employment for nearly two thousand persons. Every- one of these two thousand persons (with the exception of nine, or less than one half per cent.) is of British parentage, British birth arid British nationality. Two persons out of the nine are naturalised British subjects I and have made their home in England for over2oyears past. There are therefore only seven persons out of the two thousand, who are in a legal sense foreigners, and of these no less than four have lived in this country ex- clusively for nearly 25 years each, two of them having been brought to England in childhood. I contend that I ass justified in claiming for these four persons—two of whom have married English wives —that their long residence in our country. their close association with our Trade, our social customs and other features of our national life, have resulted in their becoming as thoroughly British in sf3ntimeat and conduct as tho most insular amongst us. The "largo number of foreigners " alleged by the Conservative placard to be employed by the firm with which the Liberal candidate is connected is therefore only three in two thousand. Two ! of the three foreigners referred to are chemists who , are exclusively engaged in research work in our lahora- -1 tory andthe third was appointed for the purpose of introducing a process in which at that time our Continental neighbours were far ahead of us. The policy of the Company has enabled us to manu- facture certain important ingredients in the production and distribution of which employment has been provided for hundreds of additional workpeople. If then, he is a" benefactor of his race who causes a single blade of grass to grow where none grew before," the people of Warrington will resent the ieaplication sought to be conveyed by the placard to which I have referred, and instead of condiimning our policy will warmly appreciate the fact that it has resulted in finding permanent employment for nearly two thousand persons and in the distribution of their earnings amongst the shopkeepers and property owners of Warrington to the extent of thousands of pounds every week.—l am Sir, yours truly.</p>
      </div>
