      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>HENRY ROBERTS, Managinc Director ofio3oph Croatiald And Sons, Ltd., Btnk Quay, WArrington, December 23th, 1905.</head>
        <p>HENRY ROBERTS, Managinc Director ofio3oph Croatiald And Sons, Ltd., Btnk Quay, WArrington, December 23th, 1905.</p>
        <p>was greatly amused to see a largo poster on the walls with these words on it, Do not vote for one who employs a lingo number of foreigners." The Tories are at their dirty work again. They hope by these means to throw the %%liking men of Warrington from the real points at present before the country, namely "Joe's ' policy,and Chinese slavery. Ido hope that the working men will not be gulled by such clap- trap and nonsense. How many men are benefited by Mr. Pierpoint ? Is the town benefited in any way ? How much money does he spend to benefit the town and relieve the taxpaying portion ? Where does be go to for the greater part of the year ? We seldom see him, or hear him, or even hear of him. I know tull well that he used to talk a good deal about Cyprus. Why he told us the other day he had been a Volunteer. Just think of it ! The Volunteers might well smile. Are the voters of Warrington satisfied with his conduct in the past to say nothing of his voting in Parliament ? T hope not. We want a gentleman to represent us who does something to benefit the town not simply ,a party. We want a man to represent us niithsomething at stake. Take the firm of Crosfield's and look at the wages they pay. They employ a vast number of men and women, boys and girls, but suppose the firm did not exist, what would those who are now employed do. How many, homes which are now happy and comfortably off would he so ? Is it not a good firm ? Where do you find a firm that has done so ranch to raise and uplift its employees and make their homes brighter and happier ? dont know of one. We have in Mr. Crosfield a very able politician whose views on all the leading questions of the day are sound and progressive. He is a gentleman—even the Tories admit that—and one who advocates reasonable sport • a liberal giver, churches and the various institutions of the town are greatly helped by him ; a man with a large heart which is full of sympathy. Think also of the rates and taxes which the thin takes:Weigh well in your minds these questions and you will then see that it is to the advantage of yourselves and the town generally to return Mr. Crosfield triihaphantly, I well remember when the 41b loaf was 10',d. If you support Mr. Chamberlains policy as advocated by Mr. Pierpoint, it may be the same again.—l remain yours, A WISRIi.ING MAX.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>MR. JOHN BURNS ON LABOUR</head>
        <p>MR. JOHN BURNS ON LABOUR</p>
        <p>IDEALS,</p>
        <p>:qr. John Burns addressed the electors,of Battersea on Wednesday night on his acceptance of a Cabinet position, as President of the Local Government Board, and the spirit in which he will approach the work of that department. In regard to municipal enterprise, he said he had already drawn a reference and was appoint- ing a committee,so that ratepayers and taxpayers would have their accounts presented to them in such a way that " he who runs may read."</p>
        <p>A. TRIBUTE, TO THE PRIME MINISTER, Alluding to his recognition by the Prima Minister and his acceptance of office. Mr. Burns said "It is a greater compliment than it seems, because if you knew him (the Prime Minister) as I know him you would see as the last ten years have proved, that be has the courage to overthrow calumny and abuse, the dogged- ness to refute misrepresentation, and by standing four- square to all the winds that blow he now earns the honour, as he is entitled to the distinction, of the King the country, and the working classes. (Cheers). The honour that has been given to me is yours ; the work is mine. The distinction is to Battersea ; the responsi- bility is to me. Friends (continued Mr. Burns), I am but, as I have ever been, the repository of your social hopes, your industrial aims, your political ideals, and as such with me the path of honour is but the road to duty, and I shall tread it as firmly and as briskly in my new office as f have ever done. (Cheers).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>MR. BURNS' IDR.tt.</head>
        <p>MR. BURNS' IDR.tt.</p>
        <p>My ideal, said Mr. Burns. is fewer workhouses and more homes, smaller charities but larger wages, more pleasure, less drink, smaller cities, larger villages, and, as a result, stronger men,stronger women, and healthier children, to whom in the years to come the word " pauper " will be a disgrace, the word " dependent " will be a shame; and to do anything but work for wages will be dishonour. That will be my programme, my ideal, and my work.—(Cheers).</p>
      </div>
