      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, December 30, 1905. NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 30, 1905. NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>the George Hotel—bas been closed and demolished to allow o? street improvements. Compensation to the amount of £2,500 was granted by the licensing authority. A great engineering scheme was completed on 29th when the transporter bridge which connects Runcorn and Widnes was opened by Sir John Brunner, M.P. Owing to seine hitch the cur ceased running soon after the formal opening, and it was not until comparatively recently that a regular service was instituted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>Rugby Football.</head>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>[By " Ovallst."]</p>
        <p>By 4.30 this Saturday afternoon Warrington will ! probably have sunk to their lowest position in the League ladder - at any rate it is to be hoped so. There is not much probability of points being gained at the expense of the Rangers, and the fourth defeat in succession should be somewhere about the turning of the lane. A chance will come with the New Year, and if the team has anything about it points should begin to accrue with fair regularity. Perhaps the ill- luck which has dogged the team right from the com- mencement of the season will take itself to regions far remote and give the club a chance of rehabilitating ! itself in the eyes of the football world. People are ! rapidly forgetting that the " Wirepullers " are the ! present holders of the Northern Union Cup,and only a brief eight months ago were "Cocks of the North."</p>
        <p>The past week has indeed been a busy one, with severe matches og Saturday, Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday, and he results cannot be considered to be anything but very disappointing. Two points were gained at the expense of St. Helens on Saturday, and in such a way as to inspire a certain amount of con- fidence. The, slu.np in Warrington stock began on Monday, when the team were nilled at Leigh, and it has continued ever since. I don't anticipate any rise before January Ist, when the visit of Dewsbury should improve the tone of the market somewhat.</p>
        <p>The most interesting match of the series was the one with Salford on Tuesday. Though the Reds have had a fairly successful season they were not expected locally to take full points away from Wilderspool. But they had the luck, and succeeded by the narrowest possible margin,much to 'Warrington's chagrin. Owing to Isherwood 's absence through a strained tendon —and following as it does upon Fish's return nothing could be more aggravating—the threequarter line has been at sixes and sevens. Jones, Jordan, Kenyon and Brooks have all appeared at centre with only moderate success. On Tuesday it was the turn of the latter pair, and they gave a very fair display though more as defensive agents than attacking forces. Isherwood's ability of making play in either direction was con- conspicuously absent and the wings suffered in con- sequence. Fish got plenty to do, and gave a good - account of hirnself,though he is far frombeing in good , runnialg trim. On the right Widdeson was idle most of the day, and people are beginning to ask when he 1, will be given a chance of showing what his capabili- ties are, He might not exist, for all the attention the 'Warrington halves and centres show him.</p>
        <p>Warrington would have recorded a meritoridus win over the Reds but for the awful mess Knight Made of a catch early on. He was caught out of place and failing to cover the ball,the ;visiting forwards snapped it up and scored. Rees, the try-getter crossed at the corner, and why he was allowed a clear run round to the posts is a question the Warrington men have pro- bably asked one another since. Lomas easily goaled, and Warrington were thns early handicapped to the extent of five points. The score remained unaltered to the interval, but afterwards the hornesters quickly drove Salford to the line and a couple of penalty goals were obtained in quick succession. Only one point divided the ;teams, and the loads strained every nerve to get ahead, Had the backs been in their best form there could have been no doubt as to the result, but the combination was poor. Twice, however, the ; Salford line was almost crossed. Once the forwards ; took the ball over, only to he robbed by Lomas, and another cross kick beat Hampson, but " Widdv " overran the ball in his eagerness. Fish also had a chance, for he came clean away with Kenyon in attendance but want of condition beat him, and he failed to give Kenyon the pass which would probably have won the match. Silford were playing second fiddle all this half,but the defence was sound.</p>
        <p>It seems paradoxical, but in my opinion Warring- ton shonld have won, and yet the worst team lost. Saliord were cleverer both at half and threequarter, and many smart things were done by Preston, John, and Hampson. The energetic Lomas was quieter than usual, but be was always to the fore in a crisis. War- rington's superiority in the second half was due to stamina and deternaination, but they never showed the same quality of football as the winners. Still, they were unfortunate not to win. It was noticeable how valuable Kenyon's turn of speed was at times in the centre, and he played a game in his new position which would justify further trial there. The taqkling was good all round, and the extra vigour i6hich ! Thomas puts into it brought him under the eye of the I referee once or twice. Forward Warrington had an I advantage in pushing and dribbling, but they did not !heel out with the celerity of their opponents, And quick " heeling " has a lot to do with the scoring, as the New Zealauders have proved.</p>
        <p>Warrington made little 'impression on the Ilunslet defence on Wednesday, though the forwards played well against a very strong lot. Hunslet put up 2 goals 3 tries to nil, the insbility of the Warrington inside men to Make headway being the cause of weakness on the attack. Just now the team's speed and:cleverness on the wings is being wasted for want of a good centre. Jones is too young and inexperi- enced to be of much value yet, but he should came on with practice. Isherwoods's return will make a difference but there will still be Wiadeson's wing short of a leader.</p>
        <p>Hunslet have on four matches in five days, while Warrington have lost three in succession. the Park- siders are in clover just now, and it should be noted that since October, 7 they have not known defeat, and have won fourteen matches off the reel, including five ties in the Yorkshire Cap Competition. In addition to this, in the four holiday matches named, they have good reasin to be prowl of the 'fact that they scored Cl points against their opponents' 5.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows : RESULTS UP TO TCESDNY, DECEMBER 26. For. Agst. Per- P. W. L. D. P. G. T. I'. G. T. P. centage Oldham lB 15 2 13126 52 208 13 6 44 8611 filmslot l5 12 3 02431 37 173 718 68 80.00 Bro'ton Rl6 12 4 02428 65 247 16 18 86 75.00 Halifax l6 10 2 4 21 20 25 115 12 9 51 7500 Leigh Hull IC.R lB 12 4 22622 25 119 22 20 100 7222 , *Leeds l5 10 4 12114 37 139 9 7 39 70400 Bradford l6 10 4 22230 52 216 15 18 84 69.75 Salford l6 11 5 02228 46 194 18 12 72 68.75 Hud'Eleld l5 9 4 22016 32 123 11 15 67 66.66! Runcorn Reighley l6 10 6 02017 28 118 17 22 100 6250 Wigan l5 9 6 01827 45 139 15 21 93 60.00 St. Helens 14 8 6 01621 32 138 20 20 109 57414 Hull l5 7 7 11521 23 117 15 21 93 50.00 Swinton l5 7 7 11513 15 71 16 21 95 5000 Wak'6eld Tl 7 7 7 31720 22 106 21 31 135 5000 I Warrington 9 810 11723 25 121 11 26 112 .1.1'73 *Bramley l2 4 6 210 7 8 33 11 16 72 4166 York l7 7 9 11523 18 100 15 26 108 4411 Pontefract 15 5 9 11119 18 92 22 at 116 36•66 Dewsbury lB 611 11313 21 89 23 29 133 3611 Castleford lO 3 G 1 710 6 38 16 26 110 3500 Barrow l4 311 0 6 6 14 54 27 51 207 21,12 Widned l2 2 9 1 5 2 8 28 15 27 111 2083 Morecambe 12 2 9 1 510 17 71 20 40 160 2083. Millorn 9 1 7 1 3 7 7 35 20 34 112 16.66 Batley l5 212 1 516 4 41 24 30 133 1666 Normanton 13 110 2 4 4 2 14 21 35 155 15•42 RochdaleH 17 113 3 510 16 68 21 53 201 1470 Brighouseß 14 0 12 2 2 9 7 39 27 49 201 714 The points are two for a win and one for a draw. The percentage is calculated upon the basis of the total points possible.</p>
        <p>TELEPHONE 382 Y</p>
        <p>SMITH __&amp; WALKER,</p>
        <p>with both the inside men away I shall think very poorly of Warrington if they do not snatch a victory.</p>
        <p>The tearn for the Match at Broughton to-day has been strenthened by the inclusion of Isherwood and Kenyon at centre, but I should have preferred Brooks at half- back. The fifteen are : Knight ; Fish, Isherwood, Kenyon and Widdeson ; Hockenhall and Lloyd ; Preston, Boardman, Shugars, Thomas, Heath, Swift, Jolley and Harmer, forwards.</p>
        <p>The Rangers have decided to play Willie James and Claude James at centre in place of fiqrry and Wilson, while Horton partners S. James at half back.</p>
        <p>On Monday (sew Year's Day) Warrington are at home to Dewsbury, and ought to easily stnagthen their position. The same day, the "International " takes place at Wigan, where Fish, llockenhall, and Shugars will all be engaged.</p>
        <p>The " A " team has an important combination game with Broughton Rangers " A" at Wilderspool to-day, and should maintain the,unbeaten record. Berry has returned to the fold and the selected team is : Hallam; Jordan, Berry, Jones and Brooks ; Garside and Hewitt; Lunt, Ashton, Forster, Hartley and Fairhurst.</p>
        <p>The holidays have been disastrous to the Stockton Heath Rugby Union team who have been soundly beaten in the two matches which they played. Their opponents on Saturday were Eccles and with a weakened team the ." Heathens " lost by 29 points to 9. The defeat could not be attributed to any particu- lar department, the team altogether being inferior to the visitors' fifteen. The forwards worked well but were beaten in the pack. At half-back Holden played a soundgame. Of the three quarter lino Ferguson, . playing at centre, was the pick. Jones at full back, apart from one or two mistakes, plav6d • well, his tackling being plucky and effective. The team which visited Eccles on Tuesday was weakened by the absence of Ferguson, Holden, Cook, K. Gibbs and Daintith, who were assisting the Cheshire juniors in their match with Lancashire. With the three best forwards off the pack was thoroughly beaten, and this led primarily to the defeat of the team. A. Conley donned the red and black jersey for the first time, and played a splendid game at centre. He combined nicely with his wing man, bat owing to the " spotting " tactics adopted by the home backs, little headway could be made. To-day the Heathens visit Heaton Moor, and have a stiff match before them. With the team at full strength, however, they are hoping to stem the tide of disaster which has set in against them.</p>
        <p>(LATE HOLMES AND BOOTH), MARKET-GATE, WARRINGTON, LADIES' AND GENTLEMEN'S TAILORS. AGENTS FOR THE WELL-KNOWN N. AND C. RAINCOAT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Hockey.</p>
        <p>On Saturday Stockton Heath visited Chester, and defeated the home side by 5-2 after a very one sided game. As Chester had made no secret of their inten- tion to show Stockton Heath how hockey should be played, their weak display came as a surprise. The chief point in the game was the form shown by W. H. Banks and R. Pierpoint. On Saturday's game Banks must be classed among the best centre forwards in Cheshire. The goals for Stockton Heath were scored by W. 11. Banks 2 and R. Pierpoint 3.</p>
        <p>The second eleven although minus such playsrs as P. B. Hawtin, W. Howard, S. Baxter, H. Smith and B. Jones, managed to win by 1-0.</p>
        <p>This week Stockton 1109th play Brooklands Ist. and 2nd, and although the first eleven will be minus some of its regular players, both teams should prove too good for Brooklands.</p>
        <p>First v. Brooklands r.twa.v).—R. 0. Pierpoint goal ; E. E. Woods and E. PierPilint, backs ; J. G. Hatton, H. Kirkconnel. and E. Jones, halves ; T. Day, W. Richardson, W. H. Banks, R. Pierpoint and H. Dolan, forwards. Train leaves Arpley 1.12. Referee, W. Carter.</p>
        <p>Second v. Brooklands, (horne).--On Cricket ground. F. G. Kertland, goat ; P. B. Hawtin and E. Fairclougb, backs ; J. Houghton, E. Fairolough, and B. Guest, halves ; L. Pierpoint, W. Howard, H. Owen, A. Hodgkinson, awl W. Linnel, forwards. Referee, N. W. Bellamy. Bully-off, 2.30 prompt.</p>
        <p>Mr.T. B. Hawtin has arranged a side match with the Royal Cub for Monday aft ?rnoon. Bully-off 2.30. Latchford Ist were at home to Alt rinchara St:Margaret's on Saturday, and won by 3 goals to one. The second team were at Altrinchara, and defeated the junior " Maggies " by no less than 7-1.</p>
        <p>The South-West Lsncasliire League up-to-date is as follows : Points. P. W. L. D. For Mat. Pts. wigan 6 ... 4 ... 2 ... 0 43 27 ... 8 , Warrington Runcorn Widnes 5 ... 1 ... 4 ... 0 ... 11— 40 ... 2 Warrington Wesleyans held their annual Christmas St. Helens their many patrons, every member who turned out was assured of a prize. The run was to Newton Heformatory and back, a distance of over six miles. !Thirty-one runners faced the starter, who got them ofT the mark punctually to time. The first prize, a turkey, fell to Vose (scratch) who ran splendidly. I. Gibson and J. Smith also ran well, being 2nd and 3rd respectively.</p>
        <p>To-day Latchford Ist meet Sale " A at Latchford, and the 2nd Sale Moor at Sale. Train 1.11 Arpley.</p>
        <p>After all, Warrington should go to Broughton this Saturday with a fair prospect of success, for the Rangers have been severely handled in recent matches and are as badly off for reserves as Warrington. Bob Wilson, I regret to state, sustained a fractured collar bone at ,Halifax on Tuesday—an injury which will keep the crack centre out of the field for the most critical pia of the season. Frank Harry, the right centre, was also damaged, so that the combination of the Rangers' rear diviS'ion should be no better than Warrington's in this encounter. Wilson's absence Since the passing of the Vaccination Exemption Act there has been no fewer than 1150 exemption certifi- alone would give Warrington a chance of victory for cates granted by the Bacup magistrates. he is the greatest attacking centre in the Union, but</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-30_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>INTERNATIONAL AND INDUSTRIAL</head>
        <p>INTERNATIONAL AND INDUSTRIAL</p>
        <p>PEACE.</p>
        <p>Sermon by the Rey. H. Minim The Baptist Church, Golborne-streot, was well filled on Sunday eveniag last when the Rev. H. Donnie°, who, has been absent for two weeks through illness, occupied the pulpit and delivered an address on "Peace and Goodwill among men." At the outset the preacher- pointed out that to every nation and every age there had been entrusted a mission. The mission of Home wi.s that of empire building, the mission of Greece was. that of culture, the mission of the Hebrews was to bear witness to the spiritual principle and the religions idea, and if he did not mis-read the signs of the times the mission of this age was to bear witness to the solidarity of the human raca and the " Brotherhood of Man.' - Wealth and ancestry and position had for generations loomed large as forces but these were losing their power and were doomed to failure. They were powerless. : against the rising tide of Brotherhood which was rolling over the great civilised nations of the world. . Still there ware in existence several great obstacles to. the realisation of "Brotherhood" and one of the greatest was the great curse of war. Taking a somewhat super- ficial view it seemed as though the time of universal peace was farther of than ever, for Europe to-day was little better than an armed camp. The European nations could place into the field at short notice arr army of not less than 20 millions of men whilst the- annual cost of European armies reached the enormous. sum of 268 millions.:llr.Dunnico pointed out that in our own nation the annual cost of our navy 10 years ago was 20 millions; to-day it was 41 millions,and during the same period, the annual ccst of our army had risen. from 18 millions to 29 millions making a total increase, On this country elonelof 32 million pounds per annum. The majority of his congregation knew Something obout the horrors of-war. A few years we passed through ,an experience he hoped we would never pass through again. The country was stricken with, war fever—on 3 of the worst of all maladies. During that war they lost 50,000 lives and spent 230 millions of money and he challenged any honest and fair-minded man to say that we had received any adequate result for the sacrifice made. The late war had aggravated every social problem, demoralised and corrupted thousands of people and blighted many reputations, and every English man who had not used all his influence against it would pass to his grave with the full responsibility of that war on his soul. In read- ing the history of that war and in full view of its results it was almost impossible to avoid coming to the conclusion that patriotism had proved to be the. last refuge of the scoundrel. He was not prepared to that there never had been nor ever could be a righteous war, but speaking generally wars were the of selfishness and greed. It-would be difficult to find two wars during the whole of last century that had not been undertaken for national or territorial aggrandisement which a little tact, patience, morality and religion might not have prevented. In those wars over five millions of men had lost their - lives an average of 110 for every day of the century 1 and the national debt of European nations reached the ; enormous sum of .£1,973,934,212. Surely the time had ' I come when it was possible to substitute something better than war for settling international disputes and thus hastening the time when men throughout the world. might be at peace. The second great obstacle in the way- of `Brotherhood "was what for want of a more expres- 7 sive terra must be called the present "cut throat com- petitive system which was beyond doubt-the dominating factor of the modern Industrial World." Sometimes he- deplored the apparent bitterness of men into whose soul the iron had entered and yet he could not condemn is in view of existing conditions. For more than 50 years this nation had been piling up wealth out of all pro- 1 portion to the growth of the population and yet the result was enormous wealth on the one hand and appall- ing poverty on the other. There had been an extra- ordinary development of labour saving machines which ought to have made life easier but the opposite was true every new invention seemed to make life harder, corn- petition keener, the struggle to live more strenuous. Over 12 millions of people were on the verge of starva- tion, living in the cruel grip of perpetual poverty ; 20 millions were poor. One half of the wealth of this country was in the hands of 25,000 people ; only about one-third of the number of people in Warrington ; the other being in the hands of 39,000,000 of people. These results and the horrible social conditions, the direct outcome of such inequality were doe to the present competitive system which brought out the worst instincts of man, and which enabled-the rich to live in luxurious idleness, and the life of the poor more arduous. Surely these conditions ware not the final outcome of our growing civilisation. for somewhere or other there was a social order which did express the highest altruistic instincts of Immanity.How then could these obstacles be removed? Different people looked in different directions for the solution. Some, thought it would come through commerce. Some through vazt armies, some through education. Commerce as such would not solve these problems, said the preacher, notwitqtanding fiscal arguments because com- merce as commerce was inevitably selfish and the force- capable of solving them must be great enough to take no thought of itself. Neither would the solution come- through vast standing armies. They were ever a tempt- ation to aggression and cruelty. Education alone would j not solve it although it was an important factor. Ignorance was a soiree of many evils, yet the greatest culture haa existed alongside tyranny, corruption and sensuality. No, it seemed to him that the solution of all these problems was the spirit of Him whose birth they were commemorating. True, the process appeared slow, there was much confusion and strife amongst the classes and races, but in the midst of all towered the' Cross, the pledge of God's love and man's ultimate redemption.</p>
        <p>Warrington.—Printed and publshed by Joitv WALKI4II 8: Co., at The Examiner General Printing Works, Bewsey-street, and published by them at the same place, and also at The Etantinet..otfices, Victoria-road WIDNES: 3, George-street, Sr. lIKLM/i: 3, 13rket- street, EARLESTOWN; Church-street RIINCOIIS, Dec 30th. 1905.</p>
